window.harnessChunks["rows-fdf60279f58327c9.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T16:51:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chainreactors/cyber-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">chainreactors/cyber-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/chainreactors/cyber-harness/tree/89f8af5ea2f478bf96eedc8f1b6ba8401ce28853\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 89f8af5ea2f478bf96eedc8f1b6ba8401ce28853\">89f8af5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform a user-initiated agent task using model-selected tools and feedback, including authorized local cyber workflows. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task outputs, file/browser/native results, model/tool errors, user follow-ups and observed system state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: configured model in StandardLoop chooses task-specific tool calls, response and continuation. - Decision owner: native first-party agent loop&#39;s active LLM reasoning actor; optional JEV Reflex may replace a qualified repeated subworkflow but does not make the ordinary scanner itself autonomous. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: executable tool registry, session, bounded inbox, hook/event stream, provider adapters, extension lifecycle and configured permissions. - Closure path: user/task input → model chooses tool → native executor records actual result → model consumes observation/continues → session result/event stream. - Boundary reachability: documented aiscan agent and minimal cmd/agent compose real StandardLoop with model/provider; source code has a standard executable path, not only an example fixture or deterministic scan mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-selected tool execution and subsequent choices provide the task-specific decision, distinct from rule-only scanning or host wiring. - Evidence: agent runtime entry, minimal agent, runtime assembly. - Basis: structural supported executable-mode review; no live LLM/targets. - Confidence: high for a wired agentic path, not for task completion accuracy or safety of real-world scans. - Caveats: no autonomy claim is made about the scan --verify=off deterministic path, empty model/tool host, or unconfigured provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no first-party S2-specific interference-attenuation loop between separately viable S1 agent units is established at the selected task-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous subagent/IOA events can enter a bounded Inbox, but this is messaging/backpressure rather than shown inter-S1 disturbance negotiation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: event bus/inbox routes prioritized task messages; parent agent delegates and receives child reports. - Decision owner: deterministic queue/resource limits and the parent task agent, not a separately evidenced coordination regulator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Inbox, async subagent results, IOA messaging, host node connection management. - Closure path: messages arrive or are rejected, then agent handles next task turn; no independently operating S1 peers&#39; actions are altered in response to a concrete shared collision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: routing and queue priorities are not sufficient without an inter-unit interference witness and returned S2-specific regulation. - Evidence: architecture: inbox and subagents, scanner mode separation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium, bounded to default and explicitly supported variants inspected. - Caveats: a multi-node application using IOA may implement its own inter-unit resource conflict governance externally.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current resource/commitment regulator with distinct decision ownership is established for this single task cell. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool errors, scanner events, guardrail prompts, session requests and JEV qualification failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user configures permissions and may intervene; deterministic tool hooks enforce rules; model chooses its own task work. - Decision owner: external user for local settings, task model for work, static controls for safety; not a shared S3 manager over plural operations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: guardrail tool admission (default provider:none), session inbox, runtime config, effect journaling. - Closure path: a particular tool may be allowed/denied and a task adjusted, but no whole-system current resource bargain or returned portfolio regulation is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy screening and retry/error gates do not themselves choose common current commitments. - Evidence: guardrail default and hook, Profile assembly. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for assessed one-cell recursion. - Caveats: a separately configured multi-node portfolio could be reassessed at a higher recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently test proposed reusable Reflex capability claims against original user task constraints and recorded native execution evidence before allowing publication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: compiler-generated source may misstate result fields, native argument provenance, read/effect freshness, missing required work or task completion. - Claim being audited: that a compiler-generated Reflex program is safe and semantically faithful enough to reuse for an observed task capability; not that every ordinary pentest conclusion is independently correct. - Ordinary reporting path: foreground agent native-tool results and compiler draft/mechanism replay proof; compiler itself proposes the program. - Complementary access path: separate JEV semantic exchange reads original task constraints, full actual trajectory, native binding witnesses and candidate program without relying solely on compiler&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: the JEV reviewer receives its own typed Claim request with independent access to actual/native evidence, not the compiler&#39;s self-attestation; the compiler can only repair and resubmit after a rejection, not mark itself qualified. - Decisive decision or feedback right: typed independent reviewer returns accept/reject (compile/defer and concrete coverage checks); rejection prevents publication and enters compiler repair diagnostics. - Decision owner: separately invoked JEV semantic reviewer in opt-in jev.mode=auto compile path; native validators independently reject invalid contracts/replay. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewReflex evidence projection, native replay witnesses, bounded 64 KiB review input, typed claims, validatereflex no-real-user-tool validation, fail-closed qualification. - Closure path: compiler draft → native validation and actual-trace replay → independent JEV semantic judgment → accepted qualification/publish or explicit failed diagnostic → compiler repair/resubmission. - Who acts on findings: the compiler agent repairs rejected programs from concrete diagnostics; qualification/persistence blocks publication until the distinct reviewer and native gates accept. - Boundary reachability: cmd/aiscan explicitly installs jevext.New when enabled with credentials, worker runs compiler, validatereflex directly invokes reviewReflex before accepted and library publication; not test-only review scripts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate semantic model makes the judgment from complementary actual evidence; the draft-producing compiler cannot self-certify or bypass the gate. - Evidence: compiler gate and repair, independent semantics and concrete failure return, mode installation. - Basis: direct source contract and verified native/reviewer path, no independent live model audit executed here. - Confidence: medium-high for distinct code path and approval authority; semantic accuracy of a given reviewer verdict remains unmeasured. - Caveats: JEV is off by default; configured TypeSafe credentials and learning mode are required. This audits generated Reflex capability, not all S1 task or scan claims, nor OpenSiro independent S3 validation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: autonomously develop and validate reusable future task capability from real execution evidence, and return a qualified program to later operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated task variety, variable handles/arguments, missing native bindings, degraded or failing candidate programs and new user inputs. - External distinction: real user tasks, changed native tool outcomes and fresh evidence challenge reusable capability; this is outside the compiler&#39;s draft and not simply re-reading the model&#39;s own plan. - Future / prospective distinction: learning turns observed executions into tested programs intended for later qualifying task instances, beyond choosing the next action of the current S1 turn. - Adaptation option generated: an executable Reflex with entry applicability, parameter schema, native contracts, evidence-aware logic and reusable persistent ID; rejected versions are repaired or retired. - Decisive decision or feedback right: background compiler agent generates executable candidate and repairs from feedback; typed criteria and native validators decide admissibility; accepted Reflex is published into runtime selection. - Decision owner: first-party JEV background compiler agent for adaptation proposals, with autonomous model-driven generation; independent semantic gate remains a distinct S3 reviewer, not identical S4 ownership. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Claim generation, tool-trace capture, bounded persistent Claim/Reflex store, compiler agent, replay, semantic reviewer and atomic publication/retirement. - Closure path: observed task → generated Claim → compiler proposes/repairs executable Reflex → native/replay/independent qualification → library persist → subsequent matching tasks run validated program with current parameters/results instead of re-planning. - Path back into current capability / S3: the qualified Reflex is atomically published to the runtime library and the JEV tool hooks may execute it in subsequent ordinary Agent turns; current-operation tool admission/replay still enforces native contracts and journaled effects. No separate S3 owner is inferred from this return path. - Boundary reachability: optional jev.mode=auto/learning=auto is registered into the actual aiscan Profile; extension.Load subscribes model/tool hooks and starts background work when configured, so loop is shipped not a proposal or isolated experiment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separately driven model compiler develops executable options and repairs from external evidence, unlike static caches, installed skills or user-authored scripts. - Evidence: JEV lifecycle and learning, extension runtime hook/work, compiler validation path, persistent publish. - Basis: structural operational path plus first-party documented real/replay tests, not reproduction of paid JEV/LLM services. - Confidence: medium-high for a shipped adaptation loop, lower for generalization/per-task savings. - Caveats: mode:off is default; learning:frozen only reuses approved Reflexes and does not exercise the S4 development mode. Failures leave old definitions intact and genuine completion is not assumed from replay alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate constitutional identity/policy issue reaches a legitimate decision owner and returns to govern the running agent&#39;s viability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user authorization, target selection, tool allow/deny, API credentials and optional JEV learning choice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user/operator chooses tool, model and opt-in modes; deterministic host/guardrail enforces configured boundaries. - Decision owner: external operator for authorized cyber task and software maintainers for release policy; no first-party S5 authority within one session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration, disclaimers, guardrail, effect journal and approval controls. - Closure path: per-tool/per-mode settings constrain task execution, not identity-level S3–S4 tension resolution with returned legitimate ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safety screening or consent is ordinary operating constraint, and a security-workflow label cannot become S5 by convention. - Evidence: disclaimer and operator scope, guardrail configuration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an external institution may supply S5 at a different, explicitly declared recursion level.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6fbe6fe3c7dd93787a536b9b820adf426e25ade0/assessments/ch/chainreactors-cyber-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6fbe6fe3c7dd93787a536b9b820adf426e25ade0\">6fbe6fe &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-23T23:04:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/archimedes-run/omniHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">omniHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-23\">2026-09-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/archimedes-run/omniHarness/tree/fd71e375ff626ae99537163edd3d14b16100d786\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fd71e375ff626ae99537163edd3d14b16100d786\">fd71e37 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform admitted user work through the lead autonomous agent and subordinate task agents using files, shell/sandbox, web/MCP and other tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user objectives, repository/file state, external information, tool/build/runtime failures and iterative observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-local reasoning, tool actions, code/file changes and when additional delegated work is needed. - Decision owner: the autonomous lead/subagent model actor on the shipped runtime path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LangGraph execution, sandbox middleware/tools, task executor, guardrails, persistence, memory and tool error handling. - Closure path: user objective → lead agent chooses actions/delegation → tools/subagents execute → observations/results return → agent revises behavior until it answers or completes the task. - Boundary reachability: the published omniharness package and normal lead-agent/API path instantiate this loop directly. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic middleware transports/enforces state, while substantive operational choices remain model-owned. - Evidence: README.md; backend/packages/harness/harness.md; tasktool.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external provider/ACP internals are not inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no complete S2 mutual-adjustment loop among sibling subagents is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: subagents can execute in parallel and the lead enforces a hard batch/concurrency ceiling, but reviewed evidence does not tie sibling interaction to a concrete conflict/oscillation and returned behavioral adjustment between those S1 units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate subagent contexts, concurrency limit, batching, task status/result collection and shared parent sandbox/thread metadata. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: delegation, batching and concurrency enforcement alone do not establish S2. - Evidence: prompt.py; tasktool.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the hard concurrency limit regulates capacity but is not credited without the required inter-S1 disturbance witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current whole-run work program by decomposing complex objectives, selecting concurrent sub-tasks, assigning specialized subagents and synthesizing returned work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: excessive or poorly scoped decomposition, sequential dependencies, incomplete aspect coverage, failed/timed-out delegated work and the need to prioritize a bounded number of current sub-tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose current decomposition, subagent type/scope, batch priority under the concurrency limit, whether to continue with another batch and how to synthesize returned results into subsequent work. - Decision owner: the autonomous lead agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task tool, separate executors, hard concurrency ceiling, task status events, timeout/cancel handling and returned results. - Closure path: lead observes current objective/results → chooses the next allocation/batch → task tool creates bounded workers → worker results/failures return → lead revises the current work program or synthesizes completion. - Boundary reachability: the shipped lead-agent prompt enables this mode whenever subagents are configured; tasktool.py implements the actual subordinate execution path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime machinery enforces limits and lifecycle; the lead model owns decomposition, priority, assignment and synthesis discretion. - Evidence: prompt.py; tasktool.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is run-level S3 over subordinate workers, not installation-wide fleet governance across unrelated sessions. - Whole-system current view: the lead agent owns the admitted objective, its planned sub-task set/batches and the success/failure/timeout/results returned from every launched subordinate task before choosing the next batch or synthesis. - Current-control decision scope: decomposition, assignment, subagent specialization, batch priority, continuation after results/failures and final synthesis.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no materially independent complementary audit organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the preview verification gate can stop ordinary completion on a broken build and feed the error back for repair, but the same operational agent receives the build/dev-server error, fixes its own code and re-checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate independent audit judgment is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: preview auto-start, build/runtime error gate, sandbox execution and ordinary agent repair loop. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative S3 classification; the loop is credited as ordinary S1 feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a verifier label or deterministic gate is insufficient where producer and corrective judgment remain in the same operational path without complementary independent access/judgment. - Evidence: README.md. - Basis: explicit negative classification. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the self-verifying loop is operationally useful; function mapping prevents relabeling it as S3 merely because it verifies.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: turn task/environment feedback into reusable future capability by evolving persistent custom skills that are reloaded into subsequent lead-agent prompts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated non-obvious errors, recurring workflows, gaps discovered through substantial tool use and user corrections can leave the current reusable skill set mismatched to future work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide that observed task/environment evidence warrants patching an existing reusable skill or, in the parent mode, creating a new reusable skill after user confirmation. - Decision owner: base mode — autonomous lead agent for edits/patches to existing custom skills under the enabled skill-evolution mode; parent mode — user supplies the decisive confirmation before creation of a new skill. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: skillevolution.enabled, skillmanage, validation/security scan, change history and enabled-skill prompt-cache refresh. - Closure path: task/environment produces a reusable lesson → lead identifies an adaptation → skillmanage persists/scans the change → skill cache refreshes → later lead-agent turns/runs receive the updated skill capability. - Boundary reachability: SkillEvolutionConfig is a first-party optional runtime mode; harness.md states skillmanagetool is inserted when enabled, and the shipped lead prompt supplies the adaptation policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the security scanner deterministically constrains writes but does not choose the adaptation; in base mode the lead agent owns the patch/edit judgment. New-skill creation explicitly retains a separate user confirmation path. - Evidence: prompt.py; skillevolutionconfig.py; skillmanagetool.py; harness.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic persistent memory is not the basis; the positive finding depends on the separate skill-evolution write/reload loop. - External distinction: runtime/task evidence includes non-obvious tool/environment errors and explicit user corrections encountered while operating in the external task environment. - Future / prospective distinction: the prompt asks whether the lesson is non-trivial/recurring and worth preserving as a reusable skill for later work rather than merely fixing the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: patch/edit an existing custom skill or propose/create a new custom skill containing the reusable workflow/lesson. - Path back into current capability / S3: successful skill writes refresh the enabled-skill system-prompt cache, so subsequent lead-agent operation receives the changed reusable capability.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: persist a custom agent&#39;s identity-level SOUL/configuration when the user authoritatively asks that agent identity to be refined. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the custom agent&#39;s enduring identity, description or governing configuration may no longer reflect the user&#39;s intended role/policy for that named agent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the identity-level SOUL/configuration change that should govern subsequent turns of the custom agent. - Decision owner: the user/parent; updateagent is explicitly framed as the mechanism used when the user asks to refine the agent&#39;s identity/configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: custom-agent SOUL.md, per-user config, staged/atomic persistence, validation and prompt rebuilding. - Closure path: user requests identity/config refinement → custom agent invokes updateagent with the selected full SOUL/config change → files persist → next user turn rebuilds the lead agent with the fresh SOUL/config → subsequent operation is governed by the returned identity decision. - Boundary reachability: custom agents and updateagent are shipped first-party operating paths; the tool is available inside an existing custom-agent chat rather than only in repository development. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model transports/implements the identity change, but the documented decisive authority is the user request; therefore the positive state is parent-governed rather than autonomous S5. - Evidence: updateagenttool.py; prompt.py; harness.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary user task instructions are not S5; this finding is limited to the persistent named custom-agent SOUL/config identity path. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether the enduring named custom agent&#39;s SOUL/description/governing tool/skill/model configuration should be changed for future operation. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the parent-governed mode is owned by the user/parent requesting and specifying the refinement in the custom-agent chat. - Return-to-operation path: updateagent persists the revised SOUL/config and the next turn rebuilds the agent from those files, governing subsequent operation under the new identity/policy.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6ae8031e8f19d93a2b72ed089d0eab4587ef2aba/assessments/omniharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6ae8031e8f19d93a2b72ed089d0eab4587ef2aba\">6ae8031 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T17:56:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/earthwalker17/agent-os\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agent OS (earthwalker17)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/earthwalker17/agent-os/tree/59833d3f436bd1fd842860af2aa1c081d4b20363\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 59833d3f436bd1fd842860af2aa1c081d4b20363\">59833d3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-guided first-party Coding Agent executes concrete software-development tasks using sandboxed read/write/shell tool actions and feeds results back into a bounded execution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing source files, test/build failures, user task constraints, subprocess/tool errors and runtime-generated observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-specific project inspections, file changes and allowed tool calls; adapt actions after observed results. - Decision owner: Configured coding-agent model through first-party CodingAgentRunner; ToolRuntime and ProjectSandbox enforce actions but do not choose them. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party CodingAgentRunner, task prompts, LLM protocol, ToolRuntime, ProjectSandbox, project run state and shell/test feedback. - Closure path: Task request and workspace → LLM chooses tool/action → sandbox code/shell effect → observation/error → subsequent model step, repair or completion. - Boundary reachability: The shipped background run dispatcher calls the first-party CodingAgentRunner using registered project workspaces. The runner executes model-supplied actions against the product&#39;s own ToolRuntime (backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/background.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operating right is discretionary model/tool action selection; a deterministic executor and cost/recovery gates are supporting control only. - Evidence: backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/toolruntime.py; backend/execution/sandbox.py; backend/main.py; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Static first-party source inspection at frozen ref; not an executed provider-backed end-to-end evaluation. Mode-specific positives are not blanket claims about all installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Protect concurrently operating coding S1 task units from mutually destructive file changes and surface content-specific integration conflicts. - Distinct S1 units: Parallel coding task units, independently running model/tool role instances in one eligible team run. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Different task units attempt to integrate incompatible overlapping changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: Isolated overlays plus deterministic content/path collision detection and plan-order integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A rejected task&#39;s blocker and partial run status are returned to coordinating run outputs and future user/model action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It explicitly handles actual conflicting code edits, not only a queue, message bus, concurrency limit or worker count. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel specialist tasks can produce conflicting writes to the same repository-relative path or case-colliding path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allow one compatible shared-file integration, reject conflicting later versions and mark the affected worker result as blocked/partial. - Decision owner: Deterministic first-party wave integration policy (integratewave) and isolated patch workspace; not a separate model agent deciding merge priority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Parallel wave executor, per-task patch overlay, first-writer-wins by plan order, normalized path collision checks, safe ToolRuntime writes and integration event/blocker store. - Closure path: Independent worker artifacts → per-wave integration compares overlapping changes → retain first valid version / record incompatible later path → task blockers and partial status reach coordinator, verification and next run decisions. - Boundary reachability: The shipped team runner launches multiple role-bound units using isolated overlays, invokes integratewave at each wave, writes conflict evidence to run artifacts and limits final status when conflicting (backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/integration.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Interference attenuation is real and file-content-specific, but the resolution policy is deterministic (C), while workers remain independent model-driven S1s. - Evidence: backend/execution/integration.py; backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/planner.py; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: Static first-party source inspection at frozen ref; not an executed provider-backed end-to-end evaluation. Mode-specific positives are not blanket claims about all installations.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led run planner/director sets and revises current coding-team commitments, assigns multiple task units, dependency order and execution roles based on project task and returned work. - Whole-system current view: The executing bounded team has an execution plan, dependency graph and task statuses/integration evidence stored for coordination; does not claim a global external company-level view. - Current-control decision scope: Assignment, dependency ordering and further work in one authorized run, with user reauthorization where required. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Several concurrent units, dependency requirements, task failure, blocker/recovery and incomplete current work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a plan and role/task allocation, change current subtask sequence through model planning or bounded recovery/follow-up proposals; abort or continue work based on outcomes. - Decision owner: First-party model-driven team planner within CodingAgentRunner; deterministic wave scheduler, worker pool and user approval enforce selected plan and execution boundaries. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM planloop, ExecutionPlan task graph, role contracts, computewaves, run-status summaries, bounded recovery, interruption/cancel and task report store. - Closure path: User project goal → model-derived plan with multiple specialized tasks → worker outcomes/integration findings → coordinator tracks commitments and reports blockers, chooses/requests follow-up or recovery → next current operations. - Boundary reachability: For team-eligible code tasks, the packaged CodingAgentRunner follows model plan actions, admits a dependency/role plan and actually dispatches multiple units in waves, integrating outcomes into run/plan state (backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/planner.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses the consequential current task commitments; scheduler and recovery budget code only executes/adapts within those decisions. - Evidence: backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/planner.py; backend/execution/roles.py; backend/execution/background.py; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Static first-party source inspection at frozen ref; not an executed provider-backed end-to-end evaluation. Mode-specific positives are not blanket claims about all installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: A distinct first-party, model-based read-only Reviewer task can inspect primary repository artifacts and issue independent defect/blocker findings into the team run. - Claim being audited: That coder-produced repository files satisfy requested task constraints. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder task returns its own final action/summary and fileschanged. - Complementary access path: Read-only reviewer unit can independently open shared repository files and inspect for defects. - Independence boundary: Separate planned task unit with a dedicated review prompt, enforced non-mutating source tools and its own model reasoning, not the producer&#39;s own stdout/test output. - Who acts on findings: Coordinator stores reviewer blockers; subsequent worker task/user can repair or stop further integration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Errors in produced code or integration that are not reliably revealed by the coder&#39;s own completion report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Read actual workspace files and report concrete defects as blockers, without the authority to alter source files directly. - Decision owner: First-party model-driven reviewer role in a separately executing runner task unit, under model-planned team workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer role with enforced read-only tool allowlist, specialized review prompt, final action with blockers; dependency graph and task integration/run status. - Closure path: Prior coding artifacts become visible in shared repository → separate reviewer model independently reads source → emits findings/blockers → coordinator records them and downstream/parent work is blocked or revised. - Boundary reachability: The bundled reviewer role is planner-assignable and runs through the same real per-task model/tool engine with enforced read-only tools; it reads integrated shared repo in a later dependency wave and returns blockers to the team coordinator (backend/execution/roles.py; backend/execution/runner.py; ARCHITECTURE.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Auditing judgment is performed by a distinct model role with primary-source access, not by a deterministic parser of the producer&#39;s report or UI approval. - Evidence: backend/execution/roles.py; backend/execution/runner.py; backend/execution/planner.py; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Static first-party source inspection at frozen ref; not an executed provider-backed end-to-end evaluation. Mode-specific positives are not blanket claims about all installations.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Project memory/research and a green-run skill-patch suggestion offer plausible future capability improvement, but the prospective external adaptation function/owner is not conclusively closed. - External distinction: Search and project evidence can be gathered, but the reviewed adaptation suggestion is primarily triggered by past/current task outcomes. - Future / prospective distinction: Reusable skills affect later executions, without proof of strategic external horizon assessment. - Adaptation option generated: A bounded append-style skill patch suggestion exists but promotion is user action, not a proven independent future-design authority. - Path back into current capability / S3: Approved skills can enter the standard skills store and later prompts; the missing externally prospective source/decision prevents a positive S4 state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New external technology/project needs and repeating task failures that might demand changing future system capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The main model may research and propose reusable skill edits, but adaptation decisions and forward-looking options are user-gated or task-local. - Decision owner: Unresolved division between chat model, post-run skill-patch judge and user Save action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research channel, structured project memory, memory reconciliation, skillpatch suggestion and user-facing skills store. - Closure path: Successful execution / historical evidence → suggested skill refinement → explicit user Save may change later prompts; whether external prospective scanning drives capability adaptation remains unproven. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory, research, self-improvement and future skill suggestions alone are not a demonstrated outside-and-then foresight/adaptation loop. - Evidence: backend/execution/skillpatch.py; backend/skillsstore.py; backend/execution/memoryreconciliation.py; backend/orchestrator.py; ARCHITECTURE.md - Basis: unknown - Confidence: low - Caveats: Static first-party source inspection at frozen ref; not an executed provider-backed end-to-end evaluation. Mode-specific positives are not blanket claims about all installations.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: SOUL identity anchor, manual approvals, sandbox policies and provider credential boundaries constrain behavior, but a higher ultimate policy/governance decision cycle is not demonstrated. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Authority to revise the overall multi-project agent-system purpose and permissible conduct. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human authorizes material execution and configured boundary, but full parent governance feedback is not traced. - Return-to-operation path: Authorized requests alter individual allowed work, not verified organization-level ultimate policy evolution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Policy boundaries, unsafe shell actions, credential handling and user-desired identity of project work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User can manually authorize runs, change skill content and provide configuration; adaptive ultimate identity/policy authority and governing return remain unresolved. - Decision owner: Human user and deterministic security/approval services; a qualifying first-party overall parent S5 loop not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SOUL.md identity, user confirmation, ProjectSandbox, secrets redactor, pending execution, skills registry and run budgets. - Closure path: Human grant/deny or static policy → run/tool permission; no observed ultimate organizational identity revision prompted by feedback and returned to ongoing operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static identity anchor or per-run approval is insufficient without legitimate ultimate policy judgment and return path. - Evidence: backend/execution/pendingexecution.py; backend/execution/sandbox.py; backend/orchestrator.py; ARCHITECTURE.md; backend/main.py - Basis: unknown - Confidence: low - Caveats: Static first-party source inspection at frozen ref; not an executed provider-backed end-to-end evaluation. Mode-specific positives are not blanket claims about all installations.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cce50d20ee383fea2b9b217536e14285d34ed9c2/assessments/earthwalker17-agent-os.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cce50d20ee383fea2b9b217536e14285d34ed9c2\">cce50d2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T18:37:15+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DrSeedon/orchestra\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchestra</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DrSeedon/orchestra/tree/72d48a93210c3cf12da91f7e693a4a6aae0e40f2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 72d48a93210c3cf12da91f7e693a4a6aae0e40f2\">72d48a9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform first-party hosted model-directed software-development operations in independently executing worker sessions, including file changes, investigation and bounded tool results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unstructured software goals, varying repository states, tool/model failures, uncertain code correctness and active worker messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select task-specific coding and tool actions, observe results and revise work in the active model worker. - Decision owner: Worker&#39;s configured agent model running within first-party Orchestra-managed session; provider runtime internals excluded. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orchestra SessionManager, runtime backend adapters, built-in Harness loop, per-worker worktree/tool context, task/turn state and durable session history. - Closure path: Orchestrator assigns work → running model worker changes/checks code → tool/file observations and results → model follow-up, output and merge proposal. - Boundary reachability: First-party spawnworker constructs an actual persistent worker and registered runtime and the session has a functional in-process model/tool loop as one supported mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task-specific choices are made by a model actor with real files/results; session host and API framework enforce but do not replace those choices. - Evidence: app/mcpstdio.py; app/manager.py; app/harness/loop.py; app/runtimeregistry.py; app/session.py - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Reduce concrete inter-worker interference from concurrent code writing and unsafe integration of overlapping work. - Distinct S1 units: Worker sessions run separate model/tool loops in isolated Git worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Conflicting repository file edits, simultaneous ownership of the same directory, incompatible target branch changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: Worktree isolation, controlled scope collision handling, snapshot-pinned merge workflow and conflict reporting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Failed spawn/merge gates and conflict status are returned to orchestrator/worker for reassignment or revision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It detects and regulates actual code-edit/merge interference rather than merely carrying messages. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independently operating coding workers may claim the same path/scope and attempt incompatible branch integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reject conflicting ownership claims where enforced, isolate each worker in a worktree and refuse incomplete/conflicting merge under integration gates. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic SessionManager ownership checks, worktree and merge policy; orchestrator handles subsequent changes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worktree-per-worker isolation, owned-directory overlap checks in manager path, git merge/conflict state, snapshot identity, merge receipts and tests. - Closure path: Two worker scopes/branches → concrete overlap checks/isolation → reject or defer conflicting work and apply guarded merge → feed conflict/incomplete result back to orchestrator for further assignment. - Boundary reachability: The session creation and merge paths call actual first-party scope and worktree logic, with live overlap/conflict and snapshot checks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This attenuates collisions between multiple independent active coding S1s, but the interfering-work gate is deterministic, not itself an agentic coordinating decision. - Evidence: app/manager.py; app/workspace.py; app/mergeoperations.py; app/mcpstdio.py - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led orchestrator directs present worker commitments and merges, using active task/session status, collaboration messages and completion evidence. - Whole-system current view: bounded orchestrator&#39;s current registered worker/task/portfolio status, not all provider/company operating state. - Current-control decision scope: ongoing worker assignment, work routing, review/merge decisions and corrective session steering. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing task priorities, parallel worker statuses, partial results, stalled sessions, new code dependencies and merge readiness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and update work assignments, choose models, launch/steer workers, solicit review, initiate merge or defer based on observed state. - Decision owner: First-party orchestrator model role via shipped MCP tools, with deterministic service enforcing accepted limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Spawn/worker-list/send-message/merge MCP tools, session registry, task status, quota admission, watchdog and result/merge receipts. - Closure path: Accepted user goal → orchestrator model creates/delegates current tasks → worker progress/messages and merge/verifier feedback → orchestrator reschedules/steers/reviews/merges tasks. - Boundary reachability: spawnworker and mergeworker are real role-facing tools, not placeholder planning stages; separate workers persist and message each other across turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses consequential team commitments based on workers and primary-source evidence; server enforces hard boundaries. - Evidence: app/mcpstdio.py; app/manager.py; app/taskruntime.py; app/messagedeliveries.py; app/quotagate.py - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement production claims with independently sourced actual code/snapshot review and enforced artifact acceptance evidence before merge. - Claim being audited: A particular worker&#39;s source diff correctly completes an accepted task. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker&#39;s own chat/Task progress and reported code changes. - Complementary access path: Cross-model reviewer accesses concrete source/changes and returns a distinct review artifact; separately, the pinned merge snapshot and first-party test/oracle capture actual execution outcomes. - Independence boundary: Distinct reviewer model/vendor and version-pinned source artifacts, though organizational role is within same product. - Who acts on findings: The orchestrator receives optional review findings and may request repair; the deterministic test/oracle gate may separately stop a merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker claims task completion but output is defective, stale, inadequately reviewed or fails acceptance tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: When invoked, inspect actual code in a distinct model/vendor, record a durable reviewer receipt, and return findings for orchestrator acceptance or repair. Mandatory merge enforcement of a cross-model review is not established. - Decision owner: Separate reviewer model for substantive code judgment, backed by first-party snapshot/receipt/test gates, not the producing worker alone. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional cross-vendor codexreview and durable review receipts, separately frozen merge worker snapshot, pinned acceptance oracle, mapped independent test execution, and explicit acceptor note. - Closure path: Worker source → optionally invoked different-vendor reviewer → review artifact/receipt and findings returned to orchestrator; independently the required test/oracle can block merge, while the acceptor may document why no separate review was commissioned. - Boundary reachability: First-party mergeworker checks accepted source identity, task assignment/acceptance note and test/oracle; separate supported codexreview invokes another vendor CLI on real sources. There is no source-backed unconditional cross-model review coverage gate at this frozen SHA. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: When explicitly invoked, the reviewer observes actual source separately and issues review evidence to the orchestrator; corrective consequences depend on orchestrator/acceptor decisions rather than mandatory independent-review enforcement. - Evidence: app/mcpstdio.py; app/mergeoperations.py; app/workreview.py; app/acceptance.py; app/mergetestgate.py; app/workreview.py - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Persistent knowledge, portfolio health monitoring and model tool flexibility may improve future work, but a distinct external future-scanning/capability adaptation controller is not demonstrated. - External distinction: Current repo/project diagnostics and user instructions do not show independent foresight scanning. - Future / prospective distinction: Session continuation and long-term notes mostly affect current/future task context, not capability redesign. - Adaptation option generated: No traced first-party approved future organizational-capability alternative. - Path back into current capability / S3: Model routing can change a current worker; systemic future-option adoption remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Long-term product opportunity/risk and potential adaptations in tools, organizational topology or capability beyond current task work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unknown future-adaptation choice; orchestrator can change near-term tactics and worker models but no horizon-driven capability adoption loop traced. - Decision owner: Unresolved between human owner, ordinary orchestrator and operational watchdog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Knowledge-base topic files, portfolio watchdog, usage analytics, model routing, task persistence and system instructions. - Closure path: Current operating information persists and may influence later tasks, but an external opportunity → future capability option → adopted change returning into operations is not established. - Boundary reachability: undefined - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Long session duration, memory and choosing a worker model for a current task alone are not outside-and-then intelligence. - Evidence: app/portfoliowatchdog.py; app/nativememory.py; app/prompting.py; README.md - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Human goal/task approval, agent roles and safety constraints exist, but a full ultimate organizational purpose/identity policy decision and feedback loop is not established. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Authority to define and revise the purpose of the whole Orchestra-managed organization. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Human goal/approval and static app policy; no source-grounded autonomous or parent-governed full S5 cycle. - Return-to-operation path: Approved tasks reach workers, but identity-level policy feedback is not demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes of fundamental purpose, trust/authority and acceptable autonomous agent behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Human approves goals and sets permissions; exact policy boundaries govern spawning/merging but no overall ultimate-purpose revision right with return is reconstructed. - Decision owner: Human/operator for stated mission and risk gates; no qualifying ultimate first-party S5 loop established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orchestrator role instructions, user approval in chat, model/role access, quota and merge policy, project KB. - Closure path: User direction constrains task execution → hard gates/approval; overall purpose/policy reform and return into all operations unproven. - Boundary reachability: undefined - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human authorization of individual tasks and a role prompt do not alone constitute complete ultimate identity/policy governance. - Evidence: AGENTS.md; app/mcpstdio.py; app/quotagate.py; README.md - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0de1e3e169dc8f3ec183811ff7d9b35abffb2010/assessments/orchestra.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0de1e3e169dc8f3ec183811ff7d9b35abffb2010\">0de1e3e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T17:03:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jcarlosrodicio/opencode-agent-orchestration-kit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode Agent Orchestration Kit</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jcarlosrodicio/opencode-agent-orchestration-kit/tree/a0b1b7869368576803c58b834707c907d62d273a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a0b1b7869368576803c58b834707c907d62d273a\">a0b1b78 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce project research/specifications and source/test changes as bounded software-development outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code architecture, conflicting implementation hypotheses, failures of tests, actual file differences and changing task acceptance criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed researcher/specifier/developer agents select investigation, implementation and correction actions within delegated scope; outputs and tool effects return to the current job. - Decision owner: The selected first-party OAK-configured operational agent, executed by the OpenCode model/tool host, rather than the deterministic installation manager or a fixed static rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent permissions, task routing, OpenCode task transport, shell/edit permissions, local filesystem, tool/API runtime, task contracts and deterministic test runners. - Closure path: User objective → configured lead → direct or phased delegate → model-selected read/write/check actions → project source/spec/test output and evidence → lead/user return. - Boundary reachability: The kit&#39;s shipped opencode.json declares lead as default and the packaged lead permits task delegation to developer, researcher and specifier; these are installed roles, not illustrative agents from an example project. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remove the operational model actor while keeping permissions and scripts: no task-specific source edit, specification or corrective implementation is selected. The external OpenCode runtime executes these decisions but its generic capabilities are not counted as a separate first-party OAK organization. - Evidence: default and model roles; lead delegation contract; feature delegate ordering; developer task action. - Basis: Explicit installed agent roles and structural model/tool delegation path. - Confidence: High for source reachability, not measured code-generation quality or prompt compliance. - Caveats: OAK reuses the external OpenCode agent engine. Some commands are prompt contracts and not immutable runtime enforcement. A user can operate in a simple/manual mode that does not exercise every role.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a specific interference or oscillation between distinct operational agent units and return a settlement into their subsequent work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential parallel edit collisions, contradictory investigations/specs and incompatible agent outputs; no shipped conflict-specific inter-S1 settlement was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No S2-specific conflict/oscillation arbitration distinct from current mission assignment or final artifact quality judgment was evidenced. - Decision owner: Not established for S2 at this recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sequential phase barriers, handoff packets, branch/lease/iteration locks, explicit nonparallel /autonomous mode, optional reviewer aggregation and generic role routing. - Closure path: Work passes through lead and serial roles; no separately evidenced two-unit interference → S2 mediation → return to both units&#39; subsequent interaction. - Boundary reachability: Shipped routing and locks are reachable, but their source-witnessed role is task ordering, current-run integrity or reviewer feedback, not inter-S1 oscillation regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An LLM scheduling researcher before developer or sending a review defect back for rework is not a demonstrated disturbance-specific coordination right. Deterministic journal leases also do not choose a reconciliation strategy. - Evidence: feature barriers; autonomous parallelism prohibited; journal/lease invariants. - Basis: Source-bounded negative, not a universal claim against custom integrations. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Extra user-created conflict mediators or independently deployed agent swarms were not imputed to this packaged mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the current engineering mission&#39;s shared commitments, work-phase order, correction/retry budget and readiness for closure across its delegated operational roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Under-specified objectives, changing complexity, incomplete current phase work, failure of tests, blocked review findings and exhaustion of task-local iteration budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model-backed lead chooses which role/phase receives a work commitment, whether a current plan/scope must change, when a review finding warrants developer rework and whether the bounded mission continues, blocks or closes. In /autonomous, it sets a task-specific 1–6 iteration plan before execution. - Decision owner: The first-party configured lead agent for current mission choices; reviewer owns the content of a separate audit verdict; user owns actual external approval/merge rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Phase constraints, loop-state JSON and history, budget cap/lease, mechanical status transitions, reviewer attestation requirement, oak deliver, CI and OpenCode task dispatch. - Closure path: Current mission/task reports, scope and reviewer findings → lead selects next current-role allocation/rework/stop → task instruction reaches developer or another operational role → updated work and review signal return. Durable state constrains the decision but does not choose it. - Boundary reachability: lead is the installed default and the shipped /feature and opt-in /autonomous commands place it above the project roles with explicit review and retry feedback. This current-mission authority is available without developing a new coordinator plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing lead leaves deterministic phase and budget checks but no discretionary allocation/rework choice for the current multi-role job. This is scoped to a current engineering mission, not the entire user&#39;s enterprise or upstream OpenCode project. - Whole-system current view: Current task contract, role/phase completion, source/diff and validation return, review verdict, iteration status and blocking cause for the one mission. - Current-control decision scope: Current job role allocation, phase barriers, bounded repair commitment and completion/block decision; not global organizational finances, release policy or future platform strategy. - Evidence: lead scope and phase decisions; rework return; explicit autonomous cycle and budget; iteration decisions/stop; state enforcement. - Basis: Explicit organizational role and first-party current-mission state/return wiring; no live model execution claimed. - Confidence: Medium for current-mission scope, not enterprise-wide S3. - Caveats: A phase graph or iteration validator alone would not qualify; the positive witness depends on model-owned current allocation and correction choices in the shipped orchestration mode. /loop may instead require human approval and is not claimed as a distinct parent-governed S3 mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge the implementing agent&#39;s correctness/safety claims using original source, diff and test artifacts before current work is accepted. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Developer&#39;s incomplete or self-serving success report, undiscovered regression, unsafe change, spec violation or misleading green routine checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separate model-backed reviewer reads original artifacts, chooses causal findings and a final pass/needschanges/blocked verdict; lead returns a blocking result to developer for corrected work. In autonomous mode completion additionally requires a reviewer child-session attestation. - Decision owner: The configured reviewer agent for the audit judgment; state scripts only require and bind an attestation and do not judge semantic correctness themselves. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only reviewer permissions, adversarial review skill, explicit raw diff/source/test access, review-result schema, bounded correction cycles and oak state attest-review. - Closure path: Developer&#39;s output and routine status → separately invoked reviewer directly inspects source/diff/tests → evidence-based verdict → lead passes blocking findings to developer → revised artifact returns to another review/closure. A missing attestation prevents the opt-in autonomous loop from terminal completion. - Boundary reachability: The installed reviewer is an OpenCode subagent and /feature calls it after implementation; /autonomous explicitly requires task reviewer rather than an ordinary top-level self-review and ties approval to a reviewer child session. The positive mode does not rely on CI alone or on optional unrelated benchmark workers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer independently selects the substantive discrepancy judgment from original artifacts; replacing it with a deterministic attestation verifier leaves no semantic independent review. Source-read independence is a declared role/session boundary, not a guarantee that models are uncorrelated or that the host sandbox is tamperproof. - Claim being audited: Developer/lead claims that changed code meets task spec, tests and security expectations. - Ordinary reporting path: Developer supplies its implementation summary and validation envelope to lead. - Complementary access path: Reviewer reads the real diff, source, relevant specs and repeatable tests, excluding developer summary as primary evidence; optional adversarial probing adds different inspection questions. - Independence boundary: Distinct OpenCode reviewer subagent/context with denied edit permission and separate final-verdict authority; the same host and model family may be reused. - Who acts on findings: lead dispatches corrective work to developer, then reopens review; the reviewer does not directly write fixes. - Evidence: reviewer source policy and independent artifact access; reviewer outputs; lead return path; required distinct child review in autonomous mode; review-attestation structural gate. - Basis: Source-declared and structurally connected complementary audit path. - Confidence: Medium; no claim about actual defect-detection rate or independently proven runtime isolation. - Caveats: Small low-risk direct work may use an explicit reviewer-skipping gate; optional /review-orchestrated preflight alone is not an independent audit verdict. This positive state is for the supported non-trivial /feature//autonomous review path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would sense changes in the external/future development environment, formulate prospective organizational capability adaptations and return their choices into subsequent current operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing model/tool ecosystem, persistent user needs, future project risk and capability portfolio; this future-oriented organizational adaptation closure was not established in the assessed installed project mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party current-project S4 right evidenced. The optional /evolve generates proposals to alter the OAK harness itself, with source-repository cwd and human application approval, not the user&#39;s installed coding project&#39;s strategic adaptation. - Decision owner: No S4-specific owner at the declared target-project recursion; project user/maintainers independently choose future scope and software evolution outside this task harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research role/web tools, task memories, /evolve session evidence collection and preflight, evaluator/debugger/evolver proposals, manifests and deterministic tests. - Closure path: Research and project planning can inform today&#39;s implementation; the separately scoped maintainer /evolve can feed kit development after approval, but it is not a first-party external-prospective decision path governing the assessed installed project operation. - Boundary reachability: /evolve is shipped as an optional command but explicitly requires running in the kit&#39;s own git checkout and keeps evolution artifacts in its developer documentation; the target-project runtime does not thereby acquire a standing S4 regulatory loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Capturing previous sessions, recommending a test fix and patching a harness under a maintainer approval are not by themselves a credited future-facing S4 function at the target-project recursion. - Evidence: evolution scope and cwd precondition; manifest/approval and re-evaluation path; AHE separation from normal app features. - Basis: Bounded negative at the declared recursion; separately acknowledges a developer-side adaptive mechanism. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: A separate assessment of the OAK maintainer&#39;s development organization, rather than one installed coding mission, could legitimately revisit this function at a different boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would legitimately decide an identity/ultimate-policy-level conflict and return the decision as a binding constraint on future system operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicts about the harness&#39;s underlying mission, identity and ultimate trade-offs beyond ordinary task permission or review readiness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No autonomous or first-party parent-governed ultimate-policy judgment and return loop established for the installed project harness. - Decision owner: Humans choose request, model/provider, permission and merge policy outside this first-party S5 path; OAK&#39;s configured lead resolves current work, not its own enduring identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: System and agent prompts, permissions, explicit approval boundaries, /autonomous prohibitions on merging/deploying and immutable task contracts. - Closure path: Ordinary user authorization changes the currently allowed task action; no source-reachable identity dispute → legitimate policy authority → binding decision → subsequent operation sequence at the declared recursion. - Boundary reachability: Installed policy/permission constraints are active, but a distinct ultimate-policy deliberation and governance-return function is not part of the credited mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static rules and normal human approvals constrain operations and must not be treated as a model or parent exercising S5 discretion. - Evidence: permissions and default agent; lead limits; ordinary autonomous safety boundaries. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The user&#39;s company, project maintainers and OpenCode provider may separately exercise policy/identity authority; that is not a first-party runtime S5 path here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3ae4d74988745686d93b4c710be49c792fbae5ef/assessments/op/opencode-agent-orchestration-kit.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3ae4d74988745686d93b4c710be49c792fbae5ef\">3ae4d74 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-30T19:22:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orkas</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-30\">2026-09-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas/tree/bc21869913e30b72f83998d79c030b290d10f140\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bc21869913e30b72f83998d79c030b290d10f140\">bc21869 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-visible project work through model-owned reasoning, specialist execution and tool use, producing answers, files, research, code or other requested outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, project files/context, external information, tool/provider availability, specialist domain requirements, intermediate results, errors and blockers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose task-specific actions, tool calls, artifact changes and substantive output needed to satisfy the assigned outcome, and revise that work from observed tool/result feedback. - Decision owner: Commander or the selected model-backed named Agent/worker for the active operational outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: core-agent runner; provider selection; scoped tool surface; conversation/session persistence; workspace/path guards; dispatch/handoff bus; tool-result storage; host permission gates. - Closure path: user/project outcome → model-backed operational actor selects actions/tools → first-party runner executes and returns observations/artifacts → actor revises or completes the outcome → result returns to the user or Commander for the next operation. - Boundary reachability: the frozen README documents direct Commander work and named-agent dispatch/handoff as shipped behavior, while the first-party runner builds the model/tool loop for normal group sessions. No external orchestrator must be composed to create the operational decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed Commander/specialist while retaining deterministic runner/session/tool machinery leaves execution primitives but removes the task-specific choices about what work to perform and how to respond to results. The material operational discretion is agent-owned. - Evidence: README.md; chatcommander.md; runner.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external model providers supply inference, but Orkas owns the first-party model/tool/session operating loop and the standard distribution exposes those autonomous operational roles directly.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete same-resource interference between distinct parallel S1 agent turns sharing one conversation workspace. - Distinct S1 units: two or more parallel Commander-dispatched/nested agent turns that can independently write user-visible files into the same conversation workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent turns can request the same output path, both observe it as free and silently overwrite one another&#39;s work if probe and write are not coordinated. - Attenuating coordination relation: uniquifyPathForWrite serializes path decision plus write on a mutex keyed by the requested path; after the first write, the losing writer sees the collision and receives a suffixed path rather than overwriting the winner. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the runtime returns a &lt;file-renamed&gt; signal naming the actual saved path and instructs the actor to use that exact path in subsequent reads, references and the user-visible response. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation exists specifically to regulate an evidenced interference created by parallel S1s contending for the same workspace resource. Credit does not come from group-chat routing, a queue, plurality or the existence of shared state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: same-path concurrent writes and silent overwrite between independently running agent turns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize contenders on the requested path, preserve the first writer&#39;s artifact, assign a unique path to the conflicting later writer, and return that coordination result to the later S1. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party Orkas file-write coordination machinery. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-path fileEditLock; existence probes; suffix generation; write-under-lock; &lt;file-renamed&gt; feedback block. - Closure path: parallel S1s target the same path → mutex serializes probe+write → the first write wins and the later write is renamed → the later S1 receives the actual path and changes subsequent references accordingly, eliminating the silent-overwrite interference. - Boundary reachability: this path is wired into normal first-party write-style tools (writefile, PDF/image output paths) in the shared conversation workspace; it is not an example-only or user-composed extension. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: no autonomous agent chooses the collision-resolution policy. The host deterministically applies the coordination rule. Under the Methodology publication boundary this is a first-party constructor/runtime-owned S2 path, therefore C rather than A. - Evidence: src/main/util/uniquify-path.ts; src/main/model/core-agent/local-tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shell-side writes can bypass writefile conflict protection, so the S2 claim is bounded to the first-party coordinated write-tool path rather than every possible filesystem mutation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain whole-team current control over ownership, decomposition, sequencing, verification and recovery for the user&#39;s active project outcome. - Whole-system current view: Commander receives the visible conversation/current request, runtime injection, project context and the suspended-orchestration ledger when one exists; the ledger preserves the larger user goal, current specialist owner, handoff and resume instruction across an interactive delegation. - Current-control decision scope: decide outcome ownership, task decomposition, sequencing, verification method and recovery route; diagnose agent-reported blockers; repair recoverable host/workspace blockers; resume the original outcome; decide when work may run in parallel versus when dependent work must wait. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple candidate owners, changing current user intent, dependencies among outcomes, specialist blockers/failures, interrupted handoffs, stale/failed delivered results, and the need to preserve the larger commitment while one interaction blocks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which actor owns each current outcome and how work is decomposed/sequenced/verified/recovered within the user&#39;s existing authority. - Decision owner: model-backed Commander. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: group-chat bus; server-authoritative floor; state.json; in-flight tracking; orchestration ledger; task board; runtime/session persistence; deterministic permission and admission rules. - Closure path: current whole-goal state + returned specialist/tool evidence → Commander chooses owner/decomposition/sequence/verification/recovery → S1 work runs or is held → result/blocker returns to Commander → Commander repairs, reroutes, resumes or completes the larger outcome and updates subsequent current operation. - Boundary reachability: Commander is the default shipped orchestrator for group chat. The frozen prompt explicitly assigns these current-control choices to Commander, and state.ts persists the larger Commander-owned task across handoff rather than requiring an external supervisor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: with the bus/ledger/runtime retained but Commander judgment removed, Orkas can persist and route already-selected state but does not choose the best owner, decomposition, recovery or verification route for the active whole. The decisive S3 discretion is therefore agent-owned. - Evidence: src/main/prompts/chatcommander.md; src/main/features/groupchat/state.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: delegation alone is not the S3 evidence. Credit rests on Commander&#39;s whole-goal current decision rights and the returned blocker/result loop, with deterministic state machinery treated only as support/enforcement.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary audit path was established at the declared Orkas project/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established beyond task-local verification/QA inside individual operational workflows. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate metasystemic audit judgment over the operating team&#39;s claimed reality was found. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orkas ships several verification/QA mechanisms, including provenance-bound citation checking and feature-local content/office/video checks, but these remain part of the task&#39;s ordinary production/validation path. - Closure path: no first-party complementary audit channel was found that independently samples or reconstructs project/team operations and returns findings into Commander current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: not applicable; the function itself is not established at this boundary. - Evidence: src/main/model/core-agent/research-verify-tool.ts; resources/builtin/marketplace/agents/78900d8758bc/agent.json; eval/model-eval. - Basis: explicit + structural negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the negative state is boundary-relative. A task-local verifier can be strong assurance without being VSM S3 at the project/team recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert external/user-experience distinctions from prior activity into autonomous changes to future Commander/agent competence, strategies and learned behavior. - External distinction: user corrections, edits, preferences, domain constraints, repeated success/failure evidence and other activity signals observed in completed conversations are treated as evidence about the environment rather than merely the current task instruction. - Future / prospective distinction: the reflection loop asks which stable lessons should alter later behavior and explicitly separates durable methods/preferences from transient errors, so the decision concerns future capability rather than only resolving the just-finished turn. - Adaptation option generated: revise COMPETENCE.md, revise LEARNINGSTRATEGIES.md, and for named Agents create/patch/delete learned skills when durable evidence supports a reusable change; otherwise return nothing to save. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted metacognition is re-read and injected into later eligible model turns. The empty agent id maps Commander conversations to default, so project/team current control can be changed by this learned state on later Commander turns; named agents likewise receive their own persisted learning. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections; newly discovered preferences/domain constraints; obsolete self-assessments; repeated strengths/weaknesses; reusable recovery methods; changing operating evidence across conversations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge which observed activity contains a durable lesson, what competence/strategy/skill change should be made, and whether nothing should be saved. - Decision owner: model-backed reflection run for the relevant Commander/default or named-agent scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dirty-activity gate; cooldown and per-cycle cap; transcript builder; persistence limits/injection scan; metacognition write tool; evolved-skill store; future-turn prompt injection. - Closure path: completed activity/corrections/signals → background scheduler selects an eligible scope → reflection model reviews transcript/current self-knowledge and chooses an adaptation → host persists the change → next eligible Commander/agent turn receives the changed competence/strategy/skill context and can alter present operation. - Boundary reachability: reflection is wired as first-party background runtime behavior; default explicitly covers unbound/Commander conversations, and the normal runner injects persisted metacognition into later eligible turns. The project/team S4 claim therefore does not borrow a development-only evaluator or a specialist-only private loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic scheduling decides when reflection may run and persistence enforces limits, but the material adaptation judgment—what lesson exists and how future behavior should change—is produced by the reflection model. Removing that model leaves scheduling/storage but no prospective adaptation decision. - Evidence: src/main/features/reflection-orchestrator.ts; src/core-agent/src/evolution/metacognition.ts; src/main/features/metacognition.ts; src/main/model/core-agent/runner.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic durable memory is not the S4 basis. Credit rests on the explicit evidence-review → model adaptation judgment → durable future-turn reinjection loop, especially the default Commander path at the assessed recursion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve the project&#39;s standing goal/rules and ultimate authority to create exceptions or expand/change those rules at the user/operator parent boundary. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the project is trying to accomplish and which standing rules govern its conversations, including whether the current request creates an exception to those saved project instructions or authorizes changing them. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: the project user/operator is authoritative. ORKAS.md is explicitly user-authored standing goal/rules; the current user request has higher priority; saved project instructions/tasks/memory may be changed only with user authorization. - Return-to-operation path: user authors or authorizes the standing instructions/current exception → Orkas persists the project instructions and injects the policy block plus user-authored content into every project conversation → Commander and named project agents follow that returned policy on subsequent work, with the current request able to override it for the active turn. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicts among current user intent, standing project goals/rules, project status/memory and directive-looking stale context; attempts to widen action/policy beyond the user&#39;s authorized target/condition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define the project&#39;s standing goal/rules, authorize changes to them, and create current-request exceptions or authorize materially different/policy-expanding action. - Decision owner: human project user/operator parent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ORKAS.md project-instructions persistence; conflict-priority prompt; compare-and-set model write path; project owner scope; user-intent authority rules; runner injection into every project session. - Closure path: project-level rule/goal or exception issue arises → legitimate user provides/authorizes the authoritative instruction → first-party project store and runner return it into all relevant project sessions → Commander/specialist operation is subsequently governed by the saved instructions or current higher-priority request. - Boundary reachability: projects and project instructions are a shipped first-party mode. The frozen project module states that the USER edits the standing goal/rules, the runner injects them into every project session, and model-side replacement is conditional; the project context policy explicitly requires user authorization for stored-context changes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Commander and named project actors can carry out an authorized instruction change through the conditional tool, but they do not own the ultimate policy right. The current user request outranks saved project instructions, and the runtime requires user authorization for changing those standing rules. No autonomous S5 ownership mode was established, so the published state is parent-only P. - Evidence: src/main/features/projects.ts; src/main/prompts/chatprojectcontextpolicy.md; src/main/prompts/chatuserintentrules.md; src/main/model/core-agent/runner.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural parent-governance path. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary permission prompts, deletion confirmations and tool approvals are not used as S5 evidence. Credit is limited to the user-owned project goal/rule hierarchy and its complete return into subsequent operation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8fadac4e061d450175441ec970b87040fd57a81f/assessments/orkas.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8fadac4e061d450175441ec970b87040fd57a81f\">8fadac4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-25T04:33:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/semi-hollow/NanoHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NanoHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-25\">2026-09-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/semi-hollow/NanoHarness/tree/70a0285383630e78c31ea65023fe5e1e48b6f7dd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 70a0285383630e78c31ea65023fe5e1e48b6f7dd\">70a0285 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independently perform assigned repository-engineering outcomes through iterative model/tool interaction inside each worker environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository structure, failing tests, tool observations, intermediate code state, incomplete information, execution failures and task-specific local decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action and arguments from current context and observations, decide how to react to results and decide when the assigned operational task is complete. - Decision owner: the model-driven NanoHarness worker AgentLoop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated worktrees, tool registry/router, permissions/approvals, context assembly, step/run control, persistent threads, model gateway, memory and runtime budgets. - Closure path: subtask enters a worker → AgentLoop builds current context → model chooses a tool/action → first-party runtime executes or governs it → result returns to the loop → model chooses subsequent action or final answer → worker candidate/result returns to fanout control. - Boundary reachability: both local and LIVE multi-agent worker adapters instantiate the shipped canonical AgentLoop inside the supported fanout runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime mechanisms constrain and execute decisions but do not select the substantive repository action or response to changing observations; removing the model actor removes that discretion. - Evidence: agentforge/runtime/application/agentloop.py; agentforge/multiagent/adapters/localworker.py; agentforge/multiagent/adapters/liveagentworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate semantic staleness/dependency conflict among concurrently active worker S1s so downstream work can adjust to newly discovered producer evidence without destructive interference. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a downstream worker may continue from stale assumptions while an upstream/peer worker discovers evidence or a dependency result that changes what the downstream worker should do; overlapping write scopes also create a structural concurrent-edit/merge conflict risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide when a discovered semantic change is coordination-relevant, what READY/FEEDBACK/UPDATE evidence to send on an allowed LIVE route and how the receiving S1 should alter its subsequent work after receiving that evidence. - Decision owner: producer and recipient model-driven worker agents own the substantive coordination judgment and response. The runtime owns deterministic route/freshness validation and write-scope enforcement only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LiveHandoffCoordinator, frozen semantic routes, route/version/freshness validation, receiver inboxes, safe next-model-step injection, dependency metadata and deterministic write-scope conflict scheduling. - Closure path: distinct worker S1s execute concurrently → producer model encounters coordination-relevant evidence and calls the LIVE publish tool → first-party coordinator validates/routes/version-tags the event → receiver obtains the event as control input at the next model-step boundary → receiver model changes its subsequent action/plan; separately, conflicting write scopes are withheld from concurrent launch. - Boundary reachability: LIVE publication is exposed as a first-party worker tool and the receiver control boundary is wired into the supported LiveAgentWorker execution path, not merely demonstrated in tests or examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime cannot determine the semantic content, relevance or response on its own; those decisions disappear if the model workers are removed. Deterministic route/freshness/write-scope rules enforce the coordination channel without owning the semantic coordination discretion. - Evidence: agentforge/multiagent/application/livehandoff.py; agentforge/multiagent/adapters/liveagentworker.py; agentforge/multiagent/application/fanout.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic fanout, dependency edges and shared state are not the basis of A; the positive claim rests on the model-owned semantic handoff/response path. Write-scope serialization is constructor-owned supporting coordination. - Distinct S1 units: two or more LocalAgentWorker/LiveAgentWorker instances, each running its own canonical autonomous AgentLoop against an isolated worktree and assigned subtask. - Inter-S1 disturbance: semantically dependent concurrent subtasks can diverge when a producer discovers evidence that invalidates or updates the downstream worker&#39;s assumptions; overlapping declared write scopes can additionally create direct integration conflict. - Attenuating coordination relation: frozen LIVE routes plus typed/versioned/freshness-checked READY/FEEDBACK/UPDATE events carry producer evidence to the intended receiver; the scheduler also prevents simultaneously launching known conflicting write scopes. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: accepted LIVE events are injected at the receiver&#39;s next model-call boundary as control context, requiring the recipient model to operate with the updated evidence; write-scope gating changes which S1 may run concurrently. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the LIVE relation is explicitly tied to a concrete cross-worker semantic-staleness disturbance and changes later receiver behavior, while write-scope gating addresses a concrete concurrent-write collision. The claim does not rely on worker plurality, task edges or a generic mailbox alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: regulate the current fanout organization as a whole by deciding which commitments may run, which must wait, how shared concurrency is allocated, which candidates may be integrated and when the organization may transition to finalization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency violations, write-scope collisions, excessive concurrent workers, failed/blocked subtasks, stale LIVE prerequisites, multiple candidate outputs and incomplete integration state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit/hold current subtasks, enforce maxworkers, serialize conflicting write scopes, gate downstream work on dependency/LIVE readiness, integrate eligible candidates and decide when all current commitments are ready for finalization. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic/configured FanoutCoordinator scheduling and integration regime; no autonomous whole-system current-control agent owns these decisions in the supported runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent fanout state, subtask statuses/dependencies, running/pending sets, candidate/result records, write-scope metadata, LIVE route/freshness state, worktree integration helpers and finalizer transition logic. - Closure path: coordinator observes current fanout-wide task/candidate/dependency state → deterministic eligibility/capacity/conflict rules choose launch/hold/integrate/finalize transitions → workers and integration state are changed accordingly → updated whole-system state feeds the next scheduling cycle. - Boundary reachability: FanoutCoordinator is the shipped execution owner for supported multi-agent fanout and directly drives worker launch/integration/finalization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AdaptivePlanner is model-driven but performs initial decomposition; it does not own the live whole-system inside-and-now decisions. Those current-control decisions remain encoded in the coordinator/runtime, so C is appropriate rather than A. - Evidence: agentforge/multiagent/application/fanout.py; agentforge/multiagent/application/planning.py; agentforge/multiagent/adapters/localworker.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary single-agent pause/steer/cancel facilities are not promoted to C(P) because a first-party parent loop with fanout-wide current view and returned whole-system control was not established at this recursion. - Whole-system current view: FanoutCoordinator holds the complete current subtask plan/status set, dependencies, running/pending/completed/failed state, candidate outputs, LIVE-route readiness/freshness, integration state and concurrency occupancy for the fanout run. - Current-control decision scope: current worker admission/holding, concurrency allocation, collision avoidance, dependency readiness, candidate integration and transition to finalization/completion.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the claim that the integrated multi-worker candidate actually satisfies the task and acceptance criteria after all worker changes have been combined. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker self-reports and successful integration can miss cross-change regressions, inconsistencies or requirement failures visible only in the combined repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the integrated candidate directly and issue an autonomous PASS / NEEDSREVISION / BLOCKED judgment with findings that determines whether the fanout may report clean success. - Decision owner: the model-driven Finalizer AgentLoop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated finalizer worktree, read-only/mutation-tool removal, fresh finalizer thread, structured verdict parsing and deterministic mapping of verdict into overall fanout status. - Closure path: workers produce candidates → coordinator integrates them → separate Finalizer AgentLoop receives the integrated repository in its own read-only worktree → model directly inspects artifacts/diff and emits verdict/findings → coordinator consumes the verdict → non-PASS verdict changes the fanout terminal result/control instead of allowing ordinary success. - Boundary reachability: LocalFanoutWorkerRunner.runfinalizer(...) is directly invoked by the supported fanout path after integration and before final status is computed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime creates the isolation and parses/enforces the verdict, but the substantive audit judgment and findings are chosen by a separate model-driven agent with complementary direct repository access. - Evidence: agentforge/multiagent/adapters/localworker.py; agentforge/multiagent/application/fanout.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary worker validation and repository benchmark/evaluator packages are not needed for this mapping and are not credited as S3. The positive witness is the post-integration, separate, read-only Finalizer path. - Claim being audited: the combined integrated candidate produced by the fanout organization satisfies the original task/acceptance criteria and is coherent as a whole. - Ordinary reporting path: worker results/candidate summaries, task statuses and successful candidate integration into the staging branch. - Complementary access path: a fresh Finalizer AgentLoop directly inspects the actual integrated repository/diff in a dedicated finalizer worktree with mutating tools removed. - Independence boundary: the finalizer uses a separate thread/worktree and read-only tool boundary after integration; it is not the worker whose claim it evaluates and is not limited to worker self-report. - Who acts on findings: the FanoutCoordinator consumes the structured finalizer verdict and maps it into overall fanoutstatus; NEEDSREVISION/BLOCKED prevents a normal clean successful outcome.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established in the supported NanoHarness operating boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4; persistent memory, retries and benchmark feedback exist but do not by themselves model external/future change and generate organizational adaptation options. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for future-oriented adaptation of the runtime organization. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: long-term memory service, remembermemory, context retrieval, skills, model configuration/adaptation tests, failure analysis and benchmark/evaluation datasets were inspected. - Closure path: no standard operating path closes external/future distinction → adaptation option generation/selection → durable capability change → return into present capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model workers can remember task-relevant facts and react to current observations, but memory persistence is not prospective intelligence. Benchmark/evaluation/failure-analysis surfaces are adjacent development/evaluation systems rather than wired owners of product-runtime adaptation. - Evidence: agentforge/memory/application/service.py; agentforge/tools/builtins/remembermemory.py; agentforge/evaluation; agentforge/bench. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the NanoHarness multi-agent organization at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: execution permissions, side-effect approvals, prompts/instructions, tool policies and operator controls constrain ordinary operation but no whole-system identity/ultimate-policy issue is closed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for identity/ultimate-policy questions. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts/instructions, safety guardrails, permissions, approvals, tool authorization, skills/configuration and operator-control surfaces were inspected. - Closure path: no system-level identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent multi-agent operation was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static prompts/policies and human approval of ordinary side effects constrain S1/S3 operation; they do not constitute S5 merely because they are high authority for a tool call. - Evidence: agentforge/runtime/application/toolauthorization.py; agentforge/runtime/application/operatorcontrol.py; agentforge/safety; agentforge/context/application/systemprompt.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e302759adc1da003baea10669617b13085e93388/assessments/nanoharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e302759adc1da003baea10669617b13085e93388\">e302759 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-07T15:35:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sp1d3rx/dendrophis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dendrophis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-07\">2026-10-07</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sp1d3rx/dendrophis/tree/dbc8e7883e7473f9450865d511d91e30c58724e2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision dbc8e7883e7473f9450865d511d91e30c58724e2\">dbc8e78 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform coding work by selecting first-party tools, inspecting/modifying files, running commands/code and iterating from returned results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user requirements, repository state, tool/test failures, provider outputs, file changes and permission/sandbox outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next coding/tool action, interpret results, change approach, delegate a specialist task and decide when the requested work is complete. - Decision owner: the main Dendrophis model loop; code-writer may own a bounded delegated implementation task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChatOrchestrator, tool registry/executor, permission policy, bash sandbox, context manager, session persistence, compaction and specialist invocation. - Closure path: user input → model decision → first-party tool/subagent action → returned result → next model decision → further action or terminal response. - Boundary reachability: the standard shipped session initializes this loop and tool surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic Python code executes/persists/constrains actions, but the model chooses task-specific operations and reacts to their results. - Evidence: session/chat.py, session/tools.py, config/systemprompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: individual operations can be denied or require user confirmation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently evidenced first-party inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S2. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S2 closure. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist roles, structured messages, modifiesfiles metadata and documentation claiming sequential execution within plans/parallel where dependencies allow. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S2 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the standard invokesubagent path awaits one specialist execution and the session tool executor runs approved calls sequentially. The frozen runtime does not wire distinct concurrently viable S1 units to file/worktree ownership, conflict detection, dependency-frontier control or another concrete interference-attenuation feedback loop. - Evidence: subagents/specs/README.md, tools/builtins/subagents.py, session/tools.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: role decomposition and a statement that work may be parallel are not credited without a frozen reachable coordination mechanism.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no model-facing whole-current control loop over a live set of subordinate operational commitments is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S3 closure. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: internal subagent task ids/status map, event-bus started/finished events, todo/sidebar/status telemetry and session cancellation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S3 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: invokesubagent blocks until the specialist returns; internal status is not exposed through model-callable list/wait/steer/kill/reprioritize controls over a live worker set. - Evidence: subagents/executor.py, tools/builtins/subagents.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: observability/status data and user cancellation do not establish an agent-owned whole-current S3 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect an implementation/diff claim through a dedicated read-only reviewer context and return an approval/changes-requested judgment to the parent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bugs, races, resource leaks, security issues, silent failures, architectural smells and style/correctness defects missed by the implementing path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect direct diff/file evidence and produce structured approved, changesrequested or comment judgment with blocker/warning/suggestion findings. - Decision owner: the dedicated code-reviewer model invocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer-specific system prompt, fresh two-message review context, direct disk reads of named files, optional dedicated reviewer model, no file mutation path, structured review schema. - Claim being audited: the focal implementation/change is correct, robust and acceptable to land. - Ordinary reporting path: main/code-writer implementation result, self-verification and tool outputs. - Complementary access path: code-reviewer independently receives the diff and/or directly reads the named current files from disk rather than relying only on the implementer&#39;s narrative. - Independence boundary: separate model invocation with reviewer-only system prompt, fresh message context, no edit/write tool loop and optional distinct codereviewermodel. - Who acts on findings: the parent receives the invokesubagent tool result and can repair/re-run review before accepting completion. - Closure path: implementation/change exists → parent invokes code-reviewer → reviewer independently inspects diff/files → structured verdict/findings return as tool result → parent can accept or perform corrective work. - Boundary reachability: code-reviewer is an enumerated model-callable invokesubagent mode and the standard system prompt tells the parent to use it for diff reviews. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the substantive audit judgment is produced by a distinct review model context with direct evidence access; deterministic parsing/transport does not make the judgment. - Evidence: handlers/codereviewer.py, specs/code-reviewer.md, tools/builtins/subagents.py, config/systemprompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer invocation is reachable/agent-selected rather than an unconditional completion gate.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: select durable lessons/conventions/preferences/architecture knowledge from current experience and return it as reusable context for future Dendrophis work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated rediscovery of project conventions/architectural decisions, recurrence of prior bug patterns, stable user preferences and lessons that future coding turns could otherwise mishandle. - External distinction: current repository/user/task experience supplies project conventions, preferences, architectural decisions, bug fixes and lessons distinct from the stored long-term memory state. - Future / prospective distinction: the shipped system prompt explicitly directs the model to save those distinctions to long-term memory for later retrieval and to search memory before future tasks. - Adaptation option generated: the model decides whether a distinction is worth persisting, chooses the memory content/tags/project association, or leaves memory unchanged; later it can select which stored memory to search/recall. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which observed knowledge becomes durable future operating context and how it should be categorized for later retrieval. - Decision owner: the main Dendrophis model through the first-party memory tools. - A witness mode: during ordinary coding work the model calls savememory for a verified lesson/convention/decision; SQLite persists it across sessions; a later session either searches/recalls it under the standard prompt or the standard MemoryAssociationGenerator surfaces a relevant match and injects its summary into context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite MemoryStore, save/search/recall tools, tags/project ids, system-prompt memory policy, MemorySearcher and automatic association generator. - Closure path: current project/user experience → model judges a distinction worth retaining → savememory persists selected long-term context → later user/task turn searches or automatically associates the stored item → retrieved memory is injected/returned into active model context → subsequent coding decisions can change under the learned guidance. - Boundary reachability: memory tools are built into the standard tool surface and the standard Session constructs MemoryAssociationGenerator whenever a MemoryStore is present. - Path back into current capability / S3: stored guidance returns through searchmemory/recallmemory tool results or automatic association injection into the main model context, where it informs present operational/delegation decisions in later turns/sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: SQLite/search/association machinery stores and retrieves content, but it does not decide which project lesson/preference/architectural distinction deserves durable retention; that prospective selection and content choice belongs to the model. - Evidence: config/systemprompt.md, tools/builtins/memory.py, memory/memory.py, memory/association.py, session/session.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: project primer persistence is not needed for this positive claim; S4 rests on model-selected durable memory plus a demonstrated return path to later active context.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime first-party identity/ultimate-policy governance loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model-backed authority to resolve Dendrophis identity/purpose/ultimate-policy tensions is established. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompt, permission categories, confirmation gates, custom project prompt, configuration, sandbox and user controls. - Closure path: not applicable. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 path claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permissions, confirmation and prompt/configuration constrain ordinary action, while ultimate authority remains external user/developer configuration rather than a first-party identity-governance actor. - Evidence: config/systemprompt.md, config/defaults.py, permissions/policy.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human approval boundary is not S5 merely because the user can override/authorize an operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/284dc1b35eb63ef6d800e35b266557f5431fb72f/assessments/dendrophis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 284dc1b35eb63ef6d800e35b266557f5431fb72f\">284dc1b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T18:38:58Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/agentic-harness-engineering\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Harness Engineering</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/china-qijizhifeng/agentic-harness-engineering/tree/8b2a55d97590363fe50c3cc6b5e833b020a4bb4c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8b2a55d97590363fe50c3cc6b5e833b020a4bb4c\">8b2a55d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: solve environment-facing software tasks by inspecting a target repository, choosing shell-based edits/tests/actions, and iterating from tool results until the task is completed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous software tasks, repository state, compiler/test failures, command output, file contents, runtime errors and task-specific implementation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive next repository inspection, edit, build or test action from the live task/tool context. - Decision owner: the configured coding-agent LLM in agents/codeagentsimple, invoked through the shipped NexAU agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runshellcommand; NexAU execution loop; E2B/Harbor task environment; in-memory tracing; model/provider configuration; iteration limits. - Closure path: benchmark task/repository state enters coding agent → model selects shell action → command changes or inspects the task environment → output/test result returns to the model → subsequent action adapts until completion/termination → external verifier evaluates the resulting task state. - Boundary reachability: this coding-agent loop is the default operational harness evaluated and evolved by AHE, not a repository-development-only helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model actor leaves shell and benchmark machinery but removes the open-ended selection of implementation/debugging actions. - Evidence: codeagent.yaml; systemprompt.md; evolve.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Harbor/E2B and model inference are external dependencies; AHE deliberately wires the model actor into the first-party coding harness and closes tool results back into that actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 loop was established that attenuates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation between distinct operational S1 units at the selected recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AHE can run many benchmark rollouts and optional evolution variants concurrently, but the reviewed distribution does not establish a shared operational disturbance among them that is sensed and attenuated as S2. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2 at this recursion. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harbor concurrency; thread pools; progress/tracer locks; git worktrees for Best-of-N variants; deterministic winner selection; task/variant result aggregation. - Closure path: no S2-specific closure established. Parallel benchmark trials are independent evaluations, while Best-of-N worktrees deliberately isolate candidate adaptations and later select one; neither path demonstrates coordination feedback that changes distinct S1 units to attenuate an inter-S1 disturbance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrency and isolation are runtime/experiment support; no autonomous or constructor-owned S2 decision is needed because the required S2 disturbance/coordination relation is not established. - Evidence: evolve.py; configs/base.yaml. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generated/evolved harnesses may themselves contain multi-agent coordination; those later candidate designs are not automatically part of AHE&#39;s own frozen organizational closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system current-control loop was established that observes live operational commitments/resources/priorities and makes bounded present-time interventions across the selected organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the orchestrator observes evaluation status, scores, timeouts and historical regressions, but its consequential changes are primarily future harness adaptations rather than regulation of live coding-task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. Target pass rate, maximum iterations, concurrency and timeouts are configured thresholds/enforcement rather than a discretionary whole-current decision right. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration loop; current pass-rate/statistics; task stability; experiment/job timeouts; configured concurrency; best-ever bookkeeping; notifications; Best-of-N result selection. - Closure path: no S3-specific closure established. Evaluation evidence flows into Debugger/Evolve analysis and then into changes to the next harness capability; that path is credited under S3/S4. Timeout/termination and target-threshold checks enforce predefined rules rather than making an open current-control allocation/commitment judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Evolve Agent owns adaptation choices, not a distinct current-control function over live operational commitments. Deterministic orchestration supplies execution support but does not close S3 under the Profile&#39;s whole-current test. - Evidence: evolve.py; configs/base.yaml; evolveprompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: if a future AHE mode gives an agent authority to reallocate or preempt live task resources/commitments from a whole-system current view, that would warrant reassessment; no such frozen first-party closure was found here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently reconcile coding-agent operational trajectories with external verifier evidence, diagnose root cause beyond ordinary pass/fail reporting, and return those findings into the evolution decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: superficial task outcomes or the coding agent&#39;s own trajectory may not reveal why a task failed, timed out or regressed; an independent evidence path is needed to distinguish agent behavior from verifier-grounded failure reality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the likely root cause and salient failure mechanism from raw traces plus verifier evidence, producing an audit finding used by the subsequent harness-change decision. - Decision owner: the Agent Debugger QA LLM invoked through bundled adb ask; it is a separate analysis actor from the audited coding-agent LLM path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled Agent Debugger source; per-task trace collection; external verifier reward/test-output collection; separate debugger LLM configuration; analysis overview/detail artifacts; query injection into Evolve Agent. - Closure path: coding agent executes task → Harbor/verifier produces reward/test output and trace is persisted → Agent Debugger independently reads raw trace plus verifier evidence → debugger produces root-cause analysis → adboverview is injected into the Evolve Agent query → Evolve Agent uses the finding to choose workspace changes → subsequent coding-agent operations run the changed harness. - Boundary reachability: agentdebugger.enabled: true in the shipped base configuration, bundled source can be installed automatically when adb is absent, and runsingleexperiment directly invokes the analysis before evolution. - Claim being audited: the operational account implicit in the coding-agent trajectory and ordinary benchmark outcome — whether/why the performed behavior actually satisfies the task, times out, or causes a verifier-grounded failure/regression. - Ordinary reporting path: Harbor statistics expose pass/fail/exception/reward and AHE records the coding-agent trace; these results alone feed iteration statistics and history. - Complementary access path: Agent Debugger receives the raw/cleaned coding-agent trace together with verifier/test-stdout.txt and reward evidence; AHE explicitly notes that this external verifier output is produced after the agent finishes and is never seen by the coding agent. - Independence boundary: the Debugger runs as a separate QA-agent process/configuration over persisted evidence and writes analysis artifacts, not the audited coding workspace. Its evidence access includes external verifier output unavailable to the S1 actor; the Evolve Agent remains the separate actor that can modify the harness after receiving the finding. - Who acts on findings: the Evolve Agent; AHE inserts Debugger analysis into section 4 of the evolution query, after which the Evolve Agent selects and applies evidence-backed harness edits used by later trials. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the Debugger LLM leaves raw traces and verifier outputs but removes the open-ended root-cause judgment. The deterministic code only assembles evidence and routes the finding; the audit judgment is model-owned. - Evidence: evolve.py; configs/base.yaml; agent-debugger-cli/source. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external verifier logic itself is not credited as AHE S3 ownership; the credited audit is AHE&#39;s distinct Debugger judgment over trace plus verifier-ground-truth evidence and its wired return to the Evolve Agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: sense external performance and current/future-relevant harness techniques, generate adaptation options, and autonomously install persistent harness changes that alter capability in later coding operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: benchmark failures/regressions, unstable task behavior, ineffective prior changes, evolving framework internals, and newly observed coding-agent architectures/tools/ablations can make the current harness inadequate for future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which prompt/tool/middleware/skill/sub-agent/memory changes to make, retain, revise or roll back based on evaluation, debugger, historical and research evidence. - Decision owner: the Evolve Agent LLM for substantive adaptation choices; Explore-Agent LLMs autonomously produce external/source knowledge used as adaptation evidence. Optional Best-of-N selection deterministically chooses among already agent-generated variants by measured performance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: evaluation statistics and task flips; changeevaluation.json; best-ever/history data; Agent Debugger findings; Explore Agent web/source research; registered research skills; writable evolution workspace; git commits/tags/worktrees; subsequent Harbor evaluation. - Closure path: current harness runs external benchmark tasks → AHE records performance/traces and Debugger findings while Explore Agent gathers source/web evidence → Evolve Agent synthesizes this evidence into concrete harness edits → first-party tools write those edits into the persistent workspace → next iteration executes the changed coding harness → new outcomes validate, revise or trigger rollback/pivot decisions. - Boundary reachability: Explore Agent and Agent Debugger are enabled in the default base configuration; the Evolve Agent and persistent workspace mutation are the core documented evaluate → analyze → improve loop. - External distinction: external benchmark/verifier outcomes expose task failures and regressions, while the enabled Explore Agent reads remote framework source plus current external coding-agent architecture/benchmark/web sources. - Future / prospective distinction: Explore Agent is instructed to search recent state-of-the-art designs and ablations for implementable techniques; cross-iteration change evaluation, stability and regression evidence also distinguish which adaptations are likely to improve rather than damage later task capability. - Adaptation option generated: the Evolve Agent may create or modify system prompts, tool descriptions/implementations, middleware, skills, sub-agents and long-term memory, and may choose rollback/pivot when prior changes are harmful or ineffective. - Path back into current capability / S3: chosen edits are written into AHE&#39;s persistent workspace, committed/tagged, and that workspace becomes the coding-agent harness evaluated on the next iteration. Thus the prospective decision changes the capability currently available to later S1 operations even though no separate S3 loop is credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator supplies evidence and writable boundaries, but the Evolve Agent makes the open-ended semantic choice of what capability change to implement. Removing that model actor leaves metrics and files but not materially the same adaptation decision. - Evidence: README.md; evolve.py; evolveprompt.md; configs/base.yaml; exploreagent/run.py; webagent/prompt.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external benchmark, web and source systems provide evidence rather than owning AHE&#39;s adaptation decision. Best-of-N is optional and not required for the S4 result; the ordinary default evolution loop already closes adaptation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop with legitimate ultimate authority and return-to-operation closure was established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: AHE has an explicit pass@1 optimization objective, protected prompt rules, workspace write boundaries and model/config restrictions, but these are authored constraints on evolution rather than a runtime identity-policy adjudication loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S5-specific right was found to resolve an identity/ultimate-policy issue or to change the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose/constitution at runtime. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configured targetpassrate; Evolve-Agent prompt constraints; write-only-within-workspace rule; prohibition on changing LLM config; protected original system-prompt rules; static strategy constraints; experiment configuration. - Closure path: no S5-specific escalation-and-return path established. The Evolve Agent optimizes within the existing pass@1 objective and authored boundaries; it does not escalate an unresolved identity/ultimate-policy question to a legitimate ultimate authority whose ruling returns into later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous optimization inside a fixed objective is S4-capability adaptation, not ownership of ultimate policy. Static operator-authored constraints remain configuration even when strongly enforced. - Evidence: evolveprompt.md; configs/base.yaml; evolve.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a human running AHE can edit configuration or source out of band, but generic maintainer authority is not a first-party operational S5 closure path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f441a0b21a7198cd49ebb24082c1bf643b5bc21a/assessments/ahe.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f441a0b21a7198cd49ebb24082c1bf643b5bc21a\">f441a0b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T18:08:16+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HaseebKhalid1507/SynapsCLI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SynapsCLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HaseebKhalid1507/SynapsCLI/tree/62853b06777fcc16db4b142d535a99e23da4b207\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 62853b06777fcc16db4b142d535a99e23da4b207\">62853b0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: foreground and optionally spawned worker agents absorb open-ended coding/terminal task variety through repeated model decisions, tool execution and observed results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain file/process/tool results, task decomposition and changing intermediate evidence in each worker&#39;s environment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose model-requested tools and follow-on tasks from returned observations, including whether to continue or finish. - Decision owner: model-driven foreground/worker agent inside the shipped runtime; an external model endpoint supplies inference but the tool-action loop is first-party wired. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, stream events, sessions, budgets, daemon, context checkpointing and cancellation. - Closure path: goal/history → LLM tool choice → first-party tool execution → tool result added to history → next provider round / final response. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool choice and next action are not prewritten as a deterministic workflow; the model acts on feedback, including inside subagent runtime. - Evidence: crates/agent-engine/src/runtime/stream.rs, tools/subagent/start.rs and README (pinned links above). - Basis: direct source-first control-flow inspection. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: task correctness depends on the selected model, tools and user permissions; a model-less session does not exercise S1. - Boundary reachability: shipped chat/TUI/RPC foreground stream and spawned subagent stream, using the standard bundled tool registry.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete file-write interference between concurrently active autonomous work units within a shared workgroup. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent foreground/worker modifications to the same file/path, plus worker output arriving after foreground completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose worker isolation/write scopes and adjust work after a collision/reconciliation signal; worker-collect/reconcile is a separate foreground coordination act. - Decision owner: foreground LLM when it selects writepolicy for subagentstart, steers/rescopes work and decides what to reconcile; host registry enforces matching scopes and returns a refusal. This does not credit deterministic scope matching itself as autonomous judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WorkerWritePolicy, checkforegroundwrite, overlap predicate, write/edit rejection and subagent completion gate. - Closure path: foreground chooses nonoverlapping scopes → concurrent agent work → overlapping foreground write is rejected with a concrete error → model receives that result and must avoid/reconcile the collision before continuing; terminal worker result is collected and reconciled before completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: unlike mere subagent delegation, the scope choice and handling of a concrete collision remain in the model&#39;s next-action loop; host rejection is evidence of an actual structurally implemented inter-S1 interference path. - Evidence: tools/subagent/start.rs, agent-core/src/orchestration.rs, tools/write.rs, tools/edit.rs, tools/subagent/collect.rs (pinned links above). - Basis: source-structural coordination witness, without an observed live multi-agent collision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: isolated worktree/read-only modes avoid the collision structurally, and incorrect agent scope declarations may fail to protect files outside their scope. - Distinct S1 units: an autonomous foreground model loop and an independently streamed subagent model loop share a task workspace; further workers may run concurrently. - Inter-S1 disturbance: foreground write/edit can target paths that a still-running worker declares writable, risking simultaneous edits to one work product. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-chosen writepolicy scope/isolation and registry&#39;s active-worker overlap check block a colliding foreground write. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the foreground receives the failed write as a tool result and can steer, collect or reconcile worker work before retrying or rescoping. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the exact overlap predicate and file-write rejection regulate concurrent operational interference, beyond mere messaging or dispatch. - Boundary reachability: bundled subagent dispatch and file-write tools use the same orchestration policy at runtime, without a plugin or downstream workflow definition.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current commitments and accountability across active subagent work on behalf of the whole foreground task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: slow/stalled or diverging workers, exhausted concurrency/total-worker capacity, finished-but-unreconciled work and conflicting current implementation responsibilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect worker statuses and partial output, steer or resume a worker, decide which work to dispatch and whether results are reconciled with the foreground objective. - Decision owner: foreground model exercising bundled subagentstatus/steer/collect/resume tools; user may intervene, while the registry only tracks/enforces quotas and completion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model catalog/authorization, typed worker roles, worker status telemetry, runtime handle registry, budget/worker-count gates and prompt supervision guidance. - Closure path: model dispatches several current commitments → reads status/progress/completion → chooses steering/resumption or integration → collected result updates current task context and explicit reconciliation releases completion gate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host logic does not choose which task is off-track or how to revise it; the foreground actor observes all subagents and has real steer/dispatch/collect authority over their current work. - Evidence: tools/subagent/start.rs, tools/subagent/collect.rs, agent-engine/src/orchestration.rs, agent-core/src/orchestration.rs, bundled supervision prompt and stream completion gate (pinned links above). - Basis: reachable first-party orchestration tools and feedback path. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: foreground supervision is prompt-guided and optional in single-agent mode; watcher restart/budget enforcement alone is not S3 agent ownership. - Whole-system current view: foreground can inspect all of its active worker handles, states, partial outputs and collected results through the shipped orchestration tools. - Current-control decision scope: model can dispatch, set role/write policy, steer, resume and reconcile current work against the foreground outcome; host applies hard budget/limit gates. - Boundary reachability: standard foreground tool registry wires subagent supervision and orchestration, and feedback returns into the same model stream.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no standard-distribution independent complementary access that challenges an S1 claim and returns audit findings into control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime traces, status and optional reviewer subagents can reveal errors, but are ordinary task/supervision paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently select an operational claim, inspect distinct evidence and return a corrective finding to current control. - Decision owner: not supplied by the standard distribution; a foreground agent/user may request an optional reviewer, without a guaranteed independent challenge channel. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent reviewer role, partial-output/status reads, telemetry, tool events and debug logs. - Closure path: ordinary worker report → foreground inspection or optional prompted review; no dedicated independent audit access and controlled corrective return is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: typed reviewer capability and logs do not establish S3 independence or a different view of operational reality. - Evidence: tools/subagent/start.rs, tools/subagent/collect.rs, agent-engine/src/orchestration.rs, README. - Basis: bounded structural negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a specifically designed downstream reviewer workgroup may establish S3 and requires separate source evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no packaged external/future sensing → adaptation option → operational capability-change loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: models/providers, task feedback, package/plugin capabilities and crash recovery are present-task or maintenance concerns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge future environmental differences and revise the agent system&#39;s future capabilities in conversation with S3. - Decision owner: user/operator/plugin author or downstream agent application; not a first-party S4 role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider catalog, plugins/extensions, MCP, watcher config reload, memory/compaction and project prompts. - Closure path: tools/model/config can change on external instruction, but the repository does not ship a prospective model of environment and a dedicated adaptation option-to-present-control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: context persistence, plugin extensibility, task planning and deterministic config reload are not prospective organizational intelligence by themselves. - Evidence: README, src/watcher/supervisor.rs, crates/agent-engine/src/runtime/stream.rs. - Basis: bounded structural negative review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: some specific user-created long-running watchers might implement future adaptation; not inferred from runtime generality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy decision path with legitimate authority and returned governing closure is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model eligibility, session budgets, credential scope, system prompts, tool confirmations and worker write rules constrain operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the identity, legitimate authority boundaries and ultimate policy if operational/future tensions require resolution. - Decision owner: user/operator/configurator at a wider boundary; runtime enforces predefined limits and explicit session grants. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DelegationPolicy, CrossProviderGrant, EnforcementMode, credential broker, user approval, prompt and role budgets. - Closure path: user-supplied settings/grants → deterministic enforcement → agent operation; no identity-level issue is routed to ultimate authority and returned as a revised policy decision in the shipped workflow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text and runtime authorization cannot be promoted to S5 merely because they constrain agent autonomy. - Evidence: agent-core/src/orchestration.rs, README, src/watchersession.rs. - Basis: bounded structural negative review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: parent organizations may impose real S5 through an embedding deployment, outside the frozen standard-distribution boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b0dbd91d81fbf7b61e6356ed7ebb418c63a9eebf/assessments/sy/synapscli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b0dbd91d81fbf7b61e6356ed7ebb418c63a9eebf\">b0dbd91 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T19:32:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/epicsagas/epic-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Epic Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/epicsagas/epic-harness/tree/1f3823af536b1f56b3cb8af90cc8ea77ef71c4bd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1f3823af536b1f56b3cb8af90cc8ea77ef71c4bd\">1f3823a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed software task execution from request/spec through task decomposition, code/tool intervention, test observation and local repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varied feature requests, test failures, codebase changes, missing context and integration feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select each next code/tool step, diagnose a failed test or review and revise artifacts until acceptance criteria or escalation conditions are reached. - Decision owner: host model running first-party /orbit and /go policies, with spawned specialist model agents owning scoped implementation decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed plugin skills, Claude/Codex/agy agent-host tools, TDD protocol, Git worktree, test runner, persistent orbit state and Rust hook state. - Closure path: user task → generated spec/role work → model-chosen edits/tests → test/reviewer evidence → model retry/adjust/stop → PR or bounded request for user decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model agents make changing next-step decisions; Rust&#39;s storage and hook checks do not replace model-driven work. - Evidence: /orbit, /go, README, src/orchestrate/mod.rs (links above). - Basis: first-party installed protocol and executable host reachability. - Confidence: medium-high for supported mode. - Caveats: runtime depends on installed host permissions/tools and the provider; no live E2E/quality certification was performed. - Boundary reachability: plugin manifests and installation load the first-party skills, and /orbit directly invokes model-host workers and returns their observations to its stateful pipeline.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent incompatible concurrently editing builder S1 agents from modifying the same file scope without isolation or sequencing. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel tasks targeting identical files, integration hazards and duplicate build work across worker contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: serialize overlapping tasks or isolate such workers in separate Git worktrees; permit independent files to proceed in parallel. - Decision owner: constructor-prescribed file-intersection and worktree isolation rules in /go and /orbit, executed by the model orchestration rather than an unrestricted autonomous policy setter. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-task modifies list, dependency graph, worktree isolation, agent result states, integration test and multi-agent review. - Closure path: conflicting pending S1 tasks → overlap/ordering decision → deferred or isolated worker executes → integration results feed later tasks/review, avoiding clobbering simultaneous edits. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2 relation targets identified shared-resource interference, beyond generic agent messaging or delegation; the governing interference rule is constructor-owned. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing TDD builder subagents in one /go multi-task plan. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two parallel model workers edit a common file, causing merge/working-tree collision and invalid integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit Tasks modifying same files → serialize or use worktree isolation, with per-task overlap checked before dispatch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: serialized workers only run after predecessors settle; isolated workers return changed trees/results for integration and failed integration feeds the repair loop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the source names an actual concurrent-write conflict and prescribes isolation/serialization conditional on overlap, altering S1 execution to attenuate interference. - Evidence: /go conflict analysis and parallel execution table, /orbit worktree protocol. - Basis: concrete first-party orchestration constraints and returned integration path. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the rule is a skill-level scheduling prescription; no universal cross-process conflict detector or measured failure rate is established. - Boundary reachability: /orbit calls /go, which requires overlap-aware planning before spawning the installed host model workers.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: model-led whole-pipeline current control over specs, tasks, worker results, verification, retries and PR readiness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task scope, failing audits/tests, missing context, partial worker outcomes, CI regressions and crash recovery state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose direct/council mode, integrate task status, dispatch and retry workers, decide phase transition or escalate a repeated failure. - Decision owner: /orbit model orchestration persona, operating the whole current work run under first-party rules and limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted PIPELINE-.json, phase history, worktree lock, /go integration, /audit report, optional /eval, CI checks, Rust orchestration events. - Closure path: goal plus whole-run state → model selects plan and workers → reads every stage&#39;s evidence → repairs failed phases or returns final PR/report/human pause. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current portfolio decisions are model policy within /orbit; Rust state management and audits supply controls and evidence but do not author the task-level strategy. - Whole-system current view: scope, phase history, worktree state, spec/AC coverage, task result states, cumulative audit failures, optional regression results and PR/CI state. - Current-control decision scope: which model subagents and phases run next, whether to retry/replan, and when the current workflow is ready to advance to ship or return to the user. - Evidence: /orbit full phase and recovery protocol, /go integration, src/orchestrate/mod.rs. - Basis: directly specified current orchestrator decision and closure rights. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: not autonomous authority over final GitHub merge or business-wide operations; EPICORCHESTRATION advanced directives are opt-in. - Boundary reachability: /orbit is a shipped runnable entry and sequentially reads/writes pipeline state, explicitly consuming audit and worker outcomes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: strict-mode separate code, security/performance and test/AC investigators challenge the current build&#39;s claimed completion, returning independent evidence into /orbit control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missed defects, builder self-certification, false-positive pass claims, review anchoring and hidden spec/test gaps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect artifacts and determine PASS/WARN/FAIL findings that block or redirect current orchestration. - Decision owner: distinct fresh-context audit agents under the first-party audit --strict protocol, not the building agent; orchestrator then owns implementation of corrective action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: artifact-only diff/spec input, builder session-ID exclusion, blind independent mode findings, tests, lint/security checks, dedup and synthesized audit report. - Closure path: S1 output/S3 completion claim → separate blind audit modes independently inspect actual diff/tests/acceptance criteria → synthesized finding → /orbit gates ship or triggers up to three corrective S1 cycles. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are independent model reviewers using complementary code/security/test access and returning a decision into S3, not merely the same builder reading its own self-report. - Claim being audited: the build orchestration&#39;s statement that all spec requirements are covered, implementation quality is adequate and a PR is ready. - Ordinary reporting path: /go&#39;s builder-submitted DONE/test summary and /orbit&#39;s own phase status. - Complementary access path: independently scoped diff/spec inspection, full test execution and security-review paths deliberately blinded to builder narrative in --strict mode. - Independence boundary: fresh audit agent instances cannot be the builder, cannot see builder session history or other reviewers&#39; conclusions before synthesis; explicit opt-in trust-isolation protocol. - Who acts on findings: /orbit consumes synthesized PASS/WARN/FAIL; failing findings cause repair/re-audit or user escalation, rather than being silently archived. - Evidence: skills/audit/SKILL.md strict audit mode, /orbit Step 4/5 and skills/eval/SKILL.md optional regression checks. - Basis: mode-specific structural independence, complementary evidence route and mandatory corrective return. - Confidence: medium; static source evidence of supported strict mode, not a verified independent deployed audit run. - Caveats: default audit lacks the explicit strict isolation promises; S3 credit applies only to reachable --strict configuration, and external CI/human review independence is not presumed. - Boundary reachability: the shipped /audit skill exposes --strict, its three independent model reviewer modes return the report consumed by the installed /orbit verdict/retry gate; a user/engagement setting must enable strict mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: multi-session evolution adapts local skills from past failures; it does not establish prospective external-environment sensing and strategic future organizational capability decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurrent failed commands, regressions, ineffective prompts and yet-untried skill edit types. - Decisive decision or feedback right: investigate external future changes/opportunities, select strategic adaptations and return them into the current S3 operational capability. - Decision owner: Rust deterministic pattern/variant pipeline and optional host synthesis for local skill repair; no independent future-environment intelligence agent found. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-invocation reflect, historical failure patterns, adaptation landscape, skill variants, seesaw no-regression gate and rollback. - Closure path: past local observations → failure classification/proposed skill patch → test/rollback/reload next session; no distinct external future view and strategic option selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evolving a response skill from local historical errors is useful operational learning but not sufficient for VSM outside-and-then S4, even if branded &#39;self-evolving&#39;. - Evidence: /evolve, src/evolve/analysis.rs, src/evolve/planner.rs, src/evolve/seesaw.rs, hooks.json. - Basis: external-vs-internal feedback discrimination. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: future releases could add environmental scouting; the pinned revision is not credited for hypothetical paths.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: scope/permission gates, installed skill identity and human STOP choices constrain code actions but do not establish first-party ultimate identity and policy adjudication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tool use, permission failures, repeated audit failures, runtime installation variance and disputed user intent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimately decide overarching system purpose and highest-level policy conflicts, returning binding identity choices to operations. - Decision owner: external user, plugin maintainers and host/provider permissions supply rules; the harness follows them and pauses for user decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: preflight lock, user pause on three failed audits, host permission model, plugin config, worktree restrictions and PR review. - Closure path: externally given goal and rules → installed operating checks, model&#39;s bounded behavior or human pause; no first-party ultimate-policy adjudication loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ability to create a PR or edit locally evolved skills is not constitutional identity authority over user/system goals. - Evidence: /orbit user gates, README installation/governance, src/orchestrate/mod.rs user-directed pause/cancel. - Basis: separation of external mandates and software enforcement. - Confidence: high for bounded S5 exclusion. - Caveats: separate human governance may be S5 in a larger assembled organization; it is not automatically imported here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d3d893bec106736a19579244c6b6eb0843950201/assessments/ep/epic-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d3d893bec106736a19579244c6b6eb0843950201\">d3d893b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-25T16:13:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/getkimchi/kimchi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kimchi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-25\">2026-10-25</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/getkimchi/kimchi/tree/65dfefb12a177e60942dc8fde87eeabe02d006af\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 65dfefb12a177e60942dc8fde87eeabe02d006af\">65dfefb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce a code change, tests, analysis or research artifact for a bounded project task using model-directed tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar source, implementation alternatives, failing tests/builds, file content, context limits and differing task requests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The Builder chooses the actual implementation and tests, the Fixer chooses repairs from findings, and Explorer/Researcher choose task-relevant observations; their executed tools affect returned artifacts. - Decision owner: Model-backed first-party Kimchi worker personas invoked by AgentManager and independent createAgentSession calls. External model inference/SDK generic loop is dependency, not credited as a separate Kimchi governance actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model-role routing, shared workspace, tool allowlists, worker turns/tokens/time budgets, background concurrency limits, session files, provider runtime and result transport. - Closure path: Parent task/spec → first-party Agent tool → Kimchi manager creates a configured worker session → model chooses read/write/bash actions → source/tests/report or blocked result returns to parent. - Boundary reachability: Enabled first-party extensions.agents and orchestration prompt are included in the regular CLI; multi-model has a hardcoded true global default. The actor path does not require a custom application to instantiate the extension or a separate developer-implemented worker. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the worker&#39;s model-mediated decisions leaves tool execution, limits and session transport, but no owner that chooses the code to write or the corrections to implement. Do not transfer upstream pi-mono&#39;s generic agent internals to Kimchi&#39;s organizational classification. - Evidence: Builder and Fixer personas; manager spawn; worker session construction. - Basis: Positive pinned first-party path. - Confidence: High for shipped autonomy wiring, not generated artifact quality. - Caveats: Single-model can run pi-mono directly with no autonomous role decomposition; the standard distribution&#39;s default multi-model mode supplies the cited positive S1 path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate an evidenced interference or oscillation between distinct operational Builder/Fixer/other S1 units without merely commanding task phases. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Possible concurrent edits to shared files, incompatible worker assumptions, cross-worker dependency conflicts or duplicate remediation; a qualifying first-party disturbance-specific response was not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No demonstrated S2-specific discretionary right distinct from the top-level orchestrator&#39;s current work assignment/review acceptance or deterministic Ferment sequence guards. - Decision owner: Not established for S2 at the reviewed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentManager&#39;s background concurrency cap/queue, GroupJoin, planner-defined chunk order, Ferment&#39;s parallel-cohort/FSM gates and prompt instructions not to work on an agent&#39;s assigned step concurrently. - Closure path: No evidenced cross-S1 conflict → specific coordination decision → changed subsequent S1 behavior loop. Review verdict → Fixer is a current quality-control/audit correction path, not proof of autonomous mediation between colliding operational S1 units. - Boundary reachability: The coordination primitives are shipped and callable; a material S2 conflict mediator is not shown in their active call path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The orchestrator may schedule tasks and interpret reviews, but generic schedule/delegation does not establish the narrower organizational S2 function. Deterministic prevention of illegal simultaneous Ferment steps is support, not discretionary inter-unit conflict ownership. - Evidence: concurrency queue; Ferment next-action sequencer; phase sequencing instructions. - Basis: Bounded negative, not a claim that multi-agent worker collisions cannot arise. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A particular deployment may implement S2 via extra custom agents, external git/worktree coordination or operator conduct; those are not credited to this standard first-party boundary without a concrete closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Control one current coding-job&#39;s global phase selection, budgets/role commitments, correction priority and final completeness after worker and reviewer feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simple versus complex requests, failing build chunks, disputed review findings, time/token constraints and incomplete current work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The top-level model-backed orchestrator classifies the task, chooses explore/research/plan/build/review phases, allocates work to specified roles/models, decides corrective Fixer calls and stops/reports after bounded verdict cycles. - Decision owner: Kimchi&#39;s current top-level orchestrator model, furnished with Kimchi&#39;s operative system-prompt workflow and Agent tool; it is not the queue/Ferment FSM itself or a human who selected the initial task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: First-party phase/guideline prompts, named persona contracts, default roles, timestamps and reports, optional Ferment state, concurrency/token limits, review-verdict files and stop caps. - Closure path: Current user goal + known workspace/worker state → orchestrator chooses plan and worker responsibilities → Builder/reviewer reports and failures → orchestrator delegates revised Fixer/chunk work or stops with unresolved problems → revised current completion state. - Boundary reachability: Default multi-model mode injects orchestration/agent extensions into ordinary CLI; a model agent actively selects pipeline and revises present commitments. Ferment additionally supplies project-wide scoped steps/recovery but its deterministic scheduler alone is not counted as owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fixed resource caps enforce choices, while the orchestration model decides which parts of current work need reassignment/correction; remove it and only a passive state machine/agent sessions remain. - Whole-system current view: One installed coding session&#39;s user request, files, plan/spec chunks, active/finished subagents, tests and independent review verdict; not the whole getkimchi company or portfolio. - Current-control decision scope: Current project task phases, which agent/model works which chunk, remediation after findings, bounded retries, completion or transparent halt. - Evidence: classification and phase selection; review remediation/stop policy; phase role decisions; Ferment actual step state. - Basis: Positive source-evidenced agent-owned current control. - Confidence: Medium-high; model compliance is not experimentally validated. - Caveats: Prompt directives can be ignored by a provider model; supported autonomous ownership is structurally reachable, not a measured success rate. Human planning approval remains within a particular operating task, not a proven (P) whole-system parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Obtain a second evidence-reading judgment of the current implementation&#39;s source, tests and match to spec, return contradictions to project control without the reviewer making audited source edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Builder self-report errors, latent correctness/security defects, unchecked missing functionality, misleading test success and implementation/spec divergence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instantiated Reviewer model examines actual code, runs tests/lint, decides APPROVED versus NEEDSFIXES with cited problems, and writes a review report; current controller routes its findings to a Fixer. - Decision owner: The separate Reviewer agent persona and child model session, with a direct read/batch test tool surface independent of the Builder&#39;s final summary. The orchestrator subsequently controls fixes; the deterministic parsing of a verdict is not itself independent judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only-oriented Reviewer persona (read/bash/grep/find/ls plus report write), fresh child context, .kimchi/docs/review.md, no direct source edits in the review contract, feedback routing to Fixer, review/fix round caps. - Closure path: Builder work claims code/test completion → separate Reviewer reads spec and raw source and runs tests/lint → evidence-based findings file → orchestrator reads findings and invokes Fixer → source is corrected or defects are reported to user. - Boundary reachability: The default coding pipeline directs model-backed review delegation, and the shipped Reviewer has direct source/tool capability. The code permits self-review for trivial low-risk changes when the orchestrator is also reviewer; that reduced path is not the independent positive witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer chooses the substantive discrepancy judgment from firsthand source/tests, rather than copying Builder output or reporting automatic CI alone. Its duty separation is by fresh agent context and role, not necessarily by distinct model family. - Claim being audited: Builder&#39;s assertions that new code matches spec, tests pass and changes contain no material correctness/security defects. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder reports artifact paths and its own build/lint/test outcome to orchestrator. - Complementary access path: Reviewer independently reads changed source and context, can execute checks, and records findings separate from the Builder summary. - Independence boundary: Distinct child session and Reviewer persona restricted by instructions to source-read/report writing, rather than the Builder&#39;s execution context. Shell write prevention is not cryptographically enforced merely by prompt; this is behavioral role independence, not a security isolation guarantee. - Who acts on findings: Top-level orchestrator and separately tasked Fixer, not the reviewer itself. - Evidence: Reviewer direct-source contract; fresh session creation; independent review and return to fix; delegated review conditional. - Basis: Positive conditional independent source-read audit path in standard multi-model code workflow. - Confidence: Medium; independence is meaningful for source access but not guaranteed for every trivial task or provider-run behavior. - Caveats: This does not claim an external institutional auditor, distinct model weights, secure filesystem sandbox or that any sample LLM review actually caught bugs.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would detect outside/future shifts and autonomously revise the coding organization’s future capabilities and resource premises rather than just execute today&#39;s task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Prospective model ecosystem/library/environment changes and organizational future requirements; no qualifying first-party adaptation loop was established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: None established for external-prospective capability planning. - Decision owner: Not established; provider and maintainers change catalogs/models, while Kimchi records selection, memory and project-scope preferences. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Researcher/web access, memory, auto backend routing pick observation, default-model deprecation fallback, local Ferment memories/decisions and skill installation. - Closure path: Current-job research and saved observations can inform tasks but were not shown to autonomously select a prospective capability strategy and return it to future execution governance. - Boundary reachability: These are shipped first-party extension paths, yet their positive returns stop at routing, task context or configuration repair. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A server-picked concrete model and client-side capability synchronization are not a first-party model agent governing prospective organizational adaptation. - Evidence: backend pick observation; dead-model default repair; Ferment scoped memory. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operational task research and provider-side future planning may still exist externally; no ownership transferred into the assessed local harness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would adjudicate ultimate organizational mission, identity, invariants and conflicting inside/outside priorities with a binding return to operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Foundational identity and policy conflicts; task authorization and model/permissions choices are a narrower variety class. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party internal or supported parent-mode ultimate identity adjudication and closure evidenced. - Decision owner: Human user owns local task intents, resources and mode configuration, but that is not a proven S5 responsibility at the organization recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Intent boundary instructions, tool permissions, project trust, user questionnaire, phase approvals, settings and hard stops. - Closure path: User constrains or revises a task; no ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → binding identity decision → future operating changes loop at the declared organization boundary. - Boundary reachability: These mechanisms are reachable but constrain local task execution rather than governance of Kimchi&#39;s identity/purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: System-prompt invariants, task approvals and static permission gates do not exercise mission/policy discretion simply because an LLM interprets them. - Evidence: task intent boundaries; user-selected mode; parent/worker exclusion tools. - Basis: Bounded negative. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Real users or upstream maintainers may exercise S5 organizationally; their separate private governance is outside the reviewed one-session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bb01978d5553f1db429b2dc3c196ee02c0eb9764/assessments/ki/kimchi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bb01978d5553f1db429b2dc3c196ee02c0eb9764\">bb01978 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T14:24:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SAIL-Research-Lab/cheetahclaws\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CheetahClaws</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SAIL-Research-Lab/cheetahclaws/tree/ec5d091b53c70f6685f1330f509b11c3a51aa214\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ec5d091b53c70f6685f1330f509b11c3a51aa214\">ec5d091 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed coding/general work through a closed autonomous model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requests, repository/file state, tool results, API errors, context pressure, external search results and intermediate execution failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive tool/action or finish response after observing the current conversation and tool results. - Decision owner: the configured model acting as the CheetahClaws agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: active tool profiles, permission checks, quotas, retries, context compaction, parallel-safe dispatch, loop guards and persistent conversation state. - Closure path: request → model decision/tool call → first-party dispatch → tool result appended to history → later model decision → outcome. - Boundary reachability: this is the ordinary shipped cheetahclaws execution loop; no Research Lab/trading module or external child harness is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code bounds and executes actions, but removing the model leaves no actor choosing materially equivalent substantive next actions. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider internals are external; the credited autonomy is the first-party closed model/tool feedback path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive filesystem interference between distinct parallel coder operational workcells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two full-loop coder subagents working concurrently in the same repository can overwrite, dirty or otherwise interfere with the same working tree. - Distinct S1 units: independently executing coder subagents, each with its own full CheetahClaws agent loop/history and bounded coding outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent code/file mutations against one shared repository working tree. - Attenuating coordination relation: the model-callable Agent tool exposes isolation=&quot;worktree&quot;, explicitly intended for parallel coding tasks that should not interfere; the runtime creates a separate git worktree/branch and injects its path as the subagent&#39;s execution root. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the isolation decision changes where each subagent&#39;s Read/Write/Edit/Bash-style operations execute for the whole child run, so later actions occur against separate working copies; completion returns branch/worktree-associated results to the parent for later integration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: credit is not for spawning or messaging workers. It is tied to an explicit parallel-file-mutation conflict and a first-party isolation mechanism whose purpose is to prevent that interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a parallel coding child should be isolated in its own worktree rather than share the parent&#39;s workspace. - Decision owner: the parent CheetahClaws agent model invoking the model-callable Agent tool and selecting the isolation mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgentManager, git worktree creation/removal, per-child worktreecwd, background threads and result/status tracking. - Closure path: anticipated parallel coding conflict → parent agent selects worktree isolation → runtime creates distinct workspaces/branches → child S1 tool activity is redirected → parent receives separated outcomes for subsequent integration. - Boundary reachability: Agent is a shipped model-callable tool and worktree isolation is implemented by the first-party multi-agent package, not an example-only composition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: git/worktree machinery enforces separation, while the model owns the discretionary choice to invoke the S2 response for the parallel coding disturbance. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/multiagent/tools.py; cheetahclaws/multiagent/subagent.py; docs/guides/extensions.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: ordinary subagent fan-out, message queues and task dependencies are not independently credited as S2; the positive witness is restricted to the explicit worktree-interference path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party autonomous or constructor path was established for whole-system current control over multiple operational units&#39; shared resources, commitments, priorities or accountability at the reviewed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established beyond local parent-task decomposition, static budgets/permissions and deterministic workflow progression. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task state, ListAgentTasks, CheckAgentResult, SendMessage, quotas/permissions, Research Lab stage state/budgets and deterministic convergence thresholds provide useful execution control but do not establish the required S3 discretion. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main agent can delegate, inspect worker status and send follow-ups, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat worker selection/delegation/result collection as S3 by itself. Research Lab&#39;s stage transitions are largely predetermined/deterministic workflow control rather than a whole-system resource/commitment bargaining function owned by a supervisor agent. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/task/tools.py; cheetahclaws/multiagent/tools.py; cheetahclaws/research/lab/orchestrator.py; cheetahclaws/research/lab/convergence.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a developer could build a supervisor from generic task/subagent primitives, but general framework expressiveness is below the C threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge a finalized Research Lab operational artifact through a separate post-finalization quality audit and feed the finding back into corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a paper can pass ordinary per-stage producer/reviewer convergence yet remain weak as an integrated final artifact in novelty, rigor, clarity or evidence. - Claim being audited: the quality of the already-finalized report after the ordinary stage graph has completed. - Ordinary reporting path: stage producers create artifacts; ordinary stage reviewers drive producer/revision convergence; finalization writes the report. - Complementary access path: /lab iterate starts after finalization and asks a reviewer pool to score the complete final report independently across four explicit dimensions; documented model assignment prefers heterogeneous reviewer families to reduce shared blind spots. - Independence boundary: the audit is a separate post-finalization pass, uses reviewer roles rather than the producing agent, operates over the integrated final artifact rather than one in-flight stage draft, and can use different model families. It is not credited from the ordinary mandatory stage reviewer loop alone. - Who acts on findings: the reviewer agents supply the dimension scores; first-party meta-loop code aggregates them, selects the weakest dimension, rewinds to a mapped earlier stage and reruns downstream work. In daemon --iterate mode this closes unattended. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent reviewer agents judge the final artifact&#39;s novelty/rigor/clarity/evidence quality; deterministic routing translates the weakest returned judgment into the corrective stage. - Decision owner: autonomous reviewer-model pool for the audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: labiterations history, score parsing/aggregation, dimension-to-stage map, persisted artifacts, resumerun, max/target/plateau/budget guards and backlog daemon. - Closure path: finalized report → independent reviewer scores → weakest dimension → stage rewind → regenerated operational artifacts/report → later iteration re-audits the result. - Boundary reachability: /lab iterate is a shipped command and the bundled daemon automatically invokes it for backlog work queued with --iterate; no development-only evaluator is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: runtime code routes and bounds the audit, but removing the reviewer models removes the substantive quality judgment that determines which dimension is weak. - Evidence: docs/guides/research-lab.md; cheetahclaws/research/lab/iterate.py; cheetahclaws/research/lab/orchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: reviewers share possible pretrained blind spots, acknowledged by the project. Citation verification alone is not the closure witness because that stage records findings and advances; the positive path is the separate final-artifact audit with targeted rewind.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: adapt the bundled trading workcell&#39;s future decision capability using empirical outcomes from the external market under an explicit operator-owned adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the trading agents&#39; confidence/recommendation process may be systematically miscalibrated as real market outcomes reveal whether past high/low-confidence calls carried signal. - External distinction: closed paper-trade outcomes and market-relative performance provide empirical evidence about whether prior recommendations actually worked; calibration reports hit-rate/signal quality and the stacker trains on closed trades. - Future / prospective distinction: the documented workflow evaluates historical performance specifically to decide whether to change future recommendation weighting rather than merely explain the completed trade. - Adaptation option generated: train/persist the bundled LightGBM (sklearn fallback) stacker so future confidence can be overridden when historical track record argues against the LLM&#39;s confidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: operator reviews calibration and invokes /trading ml train; first-party code persists stacker.pkl; subsequent trading analyses can use the trained model to change confidence presented to current decision-making. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether empirical calibration is sufficient to train/adopt the learned stacker into future operation. - Decision owner: parent human/operator in the documented trading workflow; no autonomous first-party actor is shown owning the train/adopt decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: paper-trade persistence, calibration metrics, closed-trade feature engineering, ML training/persistence and inference hooks. - Closure path: external trade outcomes → calibration/performance distinction → operator adaptation judgment → /trading ml train → persisted stacker → future recommendation confidence altered. - Boundary reachability: trading is a bundled first-party module with documented commands/storage and a recommended monthly calibration→train workflow; it is not repository-development learning. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: market-facing analysis and BM25 memory are not themselves credited as S4. The evidenced adaptation decision that changes capability remains explicitly operator-triggered, so parent-only notation is appropriate. - Evidence: docs/guides/trading.md; cheetahclaws/modular/trading/PLUGIN.md; cheetahclaws/modular/trading/agents/reflection.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Research Lab literature search is operational research, not credited as S4; generic memory/reflection is not enough. The positive path is limited to the documented external-outcome calibration and parent-triggered capability adaptation.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime path was established for identity/ultimate-policy issues to reach legitimate ultimate authority and return as governing policy for subsequent CheetahClaws operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established at identity/ultimate-policy level. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes, user confirmations, tool profiles, quotas, lab budgets/model overrides, prompts/configuration and trading risk limits constrain operation but do not by themselves supply S5 closure. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary action approval/configuration and static policy-like limits remain below the S5 threshold; no shipped identity-level escalation/governance loop was found. - Evidence: cheetahclaws/agent.py; docs/guides/research-lab.md; cheetahclaws/modular/trading/PLUGIN.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative finding. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: users remain ultimate operators in practice, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires a first-party identity/ultimate-policy return loop rather than generic human control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/d9d42892965cd598dace641839c4d605a42fae9f/assessments/cheetahclaws.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit d9d42892965cd598dace641839c4d605a42fae9f\">d9d4289 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T19:00:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/e10nMa2k/cc-mini\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cc-mini</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/e10nMa2k/cc-mini/tree/73c5bc563093b650ea8754bf9101a7507d8328a8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 73c5bc563093b650ea8754bf9101a7507d8328a8\">73c5bc5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce software-task outcomes through autonomous observation/action iteration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code, tool failures and changing intermediate results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool/action, react to returned output and decide to conclude. - Decision owner: Main model-backed agent and first-party worker agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Engine, concrete tools, permission checks, persistence and model transport. - Closure path: Model tooluse → tool execution/result messages → next model decision; no-tool result ends the turn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model leaves tool transport but removes task-specific action selection; --auto-approve is supported, while default operation constrains writes with user consent. - Evidence: src/core/engine.py; src/tui/app.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py; src/core/permissions.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Static source evidence; no live model/provider run or empirical viability claim. - Boundary reachability: src/tui/app.py constructs Engine and first-party tools; workers use the same Engine in the shipped coordinator mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate write interference among concurrently running coding workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two write-heavy workers can modify the same local file set; research can safely overlap. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose which file areas run in parallel, sequence conflicting writes and revise worker instructions. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator under its shipped concurrency instructions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent/SendMessage tools, worker task IDs, notifications; no file lock is falsely credited as decision owner. - Closure path: Worker reports return to the coordinator, which synthesizes and sends the next scoped instruction or launches work in a different file area. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The prompt supplies a concrete coordination rule for same-file writes; the agent applies its judgment to task/file overlap, rather than a queue alone deciding. - Evidence: src/features/coordinator.py; src/tui/app.py; src/tools/agent.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Prompt-level coordination is not a hard filesystem reservation or a guarantee that all collisions are prevented. - Boundary reachability: --coordinator wires the concurrency prompt and worker tools into Engine; notification draining calls runquery on that same engine. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more tool-using workers producing research/implementation outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent write-heavy tasks on the same file set risk conflicting edits. - Attenuating coordination relation: Coordinator-directed one-at-a-time work per file set, with safe independent research/verification overlap. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Completion/failure notifications inform synthesized follow-up prompts and worker continuation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped concurrency instructions explicitly distinguish same-file writes from independent/read-only work; messages carry the chosen adjustment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the coding team&#39;s current commitments and corrective intervention against the user goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Workers fail or pursue an approach that no longer serves the current goal; wrong-direction work consumes present capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Stop misdirected running work, retain or replace failed workers, and issue synthesized corrective specifications. - Decision owner: The model-backed coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker status/results/usage, TaskStop abort, SendMessage continuation and worker creation. - Closure path: Current failure or changed-direction evidence reaches coordinator; its stop/restart/replacement decision changes which worker operates and under which instructions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses the corrective commitment intervention from team results; the runtime merely aborts or resumes the chosen worker. This is narrower than budget allocation or organization-wide governance. - Evidence: src/features/coordinator.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py; src/tools/agent.py; src/tui/app.py - Basis: structural - Confidence: high within the declared source boundary - Caveats: Only the local session/team recursion is credited. Generic user permission and interruption do not independently establish S3=P. - Boundary reachability: The supported coordinator prompt explicitly instructs failure recovery and stopping wrong-direction work; shipped worker tools implement those choices. - Whole-system current view: Coordinator receives worker completion/failure, result and usage across the local team, alongside the user goal. - Current-control decision scope: Present team commitments: continue an existing worker, substitute a fresh one, or stop ongoing wrong-direction work.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No complementary audit outside routine production verification established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker claims may be wrong; routine checks can miss defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fresh verifier instructions are ordinary research→implementation→verification workflow; review/simplify runs within normal production. No separately governed sporadic audit path is shipped. - Evidence: src/features/coordinator.py; src/features/skillsbundled.py; src/features/agents/definitions.py; src/features/agents/workermanager.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No external-and-prospective capability adaptation loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User/project information and previous mistakes change across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: KAIROS consolidates internal transcripts and preferences; coordinator research and plan mode address the current task. Neither supplies future-environment adaptation options coupled to current team capability. - Evidence: src/features/memory.py; docs/memory.md; src/features/coordinator.py; src/features/plan.py; src/core/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy decision-and-return path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permissions, tool risk and plan approval constrain work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party decisive right established for this function. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at the declared boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The cited runtime and documentation are supporting surfaces, not decision owners. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure at this boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Permission choices, plan acceptance, sandbox configuration and saved preferences govern operational actions; they do not route an identity-policy matter to legitimate ultimate authority. - Evidence: src/core/permissions.py; src/features/plan.py; src/tools/plantools.py; src/core/context.py; src/core/config.py - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b44ecd935e12f8907c46cf85993cc0793d087629/assessments/cc/cc-mini.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b44ecd935e12f8907c46cf85993cc0793d087629\">b44ecd9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-16T22:40:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/himkt/cafleet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAFleet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-16\">2026-10-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/himkt/cafleet/tree/a991fe8f818a5e65770f32db3867f50b178c2e73\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a991fe8f818a5e65770f32db3867f50b178c2e73\">a991fe8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: agent members complete assigned coding, tests or code review operations against a real repository via installed scoped role instructions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requirements ambiguity, code/test errors, tool or source feedback and local decisions about task implementation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: actual model-backed assigned Programmer/Tester/Verifier chooses substantive tool/code/test actions within role constraints and returns results. - Decision owner: external model-backed agent instantiated and role/prompt-scoped through first-party CAFleet skill and member creation; its general model internals are not owned by CAFleet. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust CLI member registration/pane creation, embedded skills/presets, durable fleet-scoped message ledger, role-specific task prompt, actual user Git repository/working branch. - Closure path: designated code/test task → first-party member spawn with role prompt → model-backed member interacts with files/tests and adjusts work → persisted cafleet message send result and artifacts → Director sees status. - Boundary reachability: Rust member create with rendered prompt and setup installed roles; supported external Claude/Codex/OpenCode CLIs required as environmental inference executors. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the operating model agent leaves only deterministic broker/pane mechanics and cannot complete coding work. - Evidence: cafleet/src/assets.rs; cafleet/src/cli/member.rs; cafleet/src/broker/members.rs; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/roles/programmer.md. - Basis: first-party shipped/invocable composition and real command implementation. - Confidence: medium-high source reachability; no live inference witnessed. - Caveats: bare broker/CLI sessions without model-backed members or design-doc skill cannot inherit these positive functions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent multiple operational monitor cells from concurrently claiming the single live fleet monitoring/wake responsibility. - Disturbance / variety regulated: duplicate monitor/wake loops could compete and flood or cross-trigger Director/member attention, confusing sibling operations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: hold/reject active monitor registration and stop monitor ticks without the valid owner heartbeat. - Decision owner: first-party Rust/SQLite monitor ownership constructor (not discretionary LLM regulator). - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: unique active-monitor index, immediate registration transaction, ownership-checked runtime heartbeat. - Closure path: two would-be monitor member registrations within fleet → unique constraint permits only one active monitor → only owner loop maintains heartbeat and issues batched wake → other candidate fails or stops, leaving unique coordination channel. - Boundary reachability: fleet create bootstraps a monitor and monitortick is the actual running monitor-loop implementation, not merely specification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic gating of competing monitor operations, distinct from messaging reliability. - Distinct S1 units: model-backed operational monitor members, potentially attempted concurrently; one retained active slot. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independently initiated monitors both try to wake/support the same live fleet and perturb active worker/Director behavior. - Attenuating coordination relation: transaction-backed exclusive monitor identity and tick ownership heartbeat. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: only the valid monitor supplies Director wake context and all other launched monitor attempts receive explicit errors/stop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it excludes physically conflicting current regulatory operations by enforcing exclusive live responsibility. - Evidence: cafleet/migrations/V8uniqueactivemonitor.sql; cafleet/src/broker/members.rs; cafleet/src/monitor/mod.rs; cafleet/src/cli/fleet.rs. - Basis: shipped code, unique constraint and monitor semantics. - Confidence: medium: narrow constrained mode; no observed live monitor collision test claimed beyond source tests. - Caveats: no evidence of automatic cross-member Git conflict suppression or worktree isolation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: decide and regulate currently pending/blocked fleet work assignments and interventions across the installed design-doc execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved tasks/tests/reviews, failed/spinning members, uneven member availability and user interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model Director decides who to spawn, which current phase/step to release or retry, how to arbitrate defects or escalate, and when work is ready for review. - Decision owner: active model-backed Director using installed cafleet-design-doc skill; broker provides command effects and read-back. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet/member list, captures, persisted message send/poll/ack, worker roles, Director rule set, monitor wake. - Closure path: current design-doc/task/member/review state → Director chooses intervention/assignment → real cafleet member create/message send/member ping commands → member actions/new reports/captures return to next Director decision. - Boundary reachability: cafleet setup installs Director/execute skills, and Rust implements the invoked CLI effects; explicit skill invocation required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Director prompt delegates whole-fleet current judgment to an active model, not simple deterministic queued routing. - Whole-system current view: active members, issue/doc state, test results, messages and observer member pane state of one fleet. - Current-control decision scope: membership and task routing, retry/review escalation, work hold, acceptance handoff and shutdown; never tenant-global ultimate policy. - Evidence: skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; skills/cafleet/roles/director.md; cafleet/src/cli/member.rs; cafleet/src/broker/messaging.rs; cafleet/src/monitor/mod.rs. - Basis: installed agent instructions plus executable effects. - Confidence: medium in supported workflow, not empirically observed autonomy performance. - Caveats: Rust broker alone does not choose present commitments; workflow is prompt-compliance dependent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separate fresh agent quality review of real code changes against design acceptance with binding rework before operator approval. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect or incomplete implementer claims, missing behavior, defects or code/test mismatch unnoticed by implementers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect the whole branch diff and quality checks, report annotated defects or approved (doc); Director follows mandatory re-review workflow. - Decision owner: a fresh model-backed Reviewer spawned separately after implementation by a model-backed Director, with read-only role constraints; human operator separately controls final publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: installed cafleet-design-doc execute Step 5, roles/reviewer.md, Director&#39;s issue-marker protocol, git diff, read-only test tools, message ACK/status, Step 6 human approval. - Closure path: code+tests complete → new distinct Reviewer reads full branch diff and design criteria → Reviewer sends source-line findings → Director dispatches fixes → reviewer rechecks without cap → only approved (doc) permits user acceptance and publication steps. - Boundary reachability: built-in setup skill installation, conditionally invoked execute skill; executable member creation, messaging, prompts and real checkout. Not a default broker-wide transaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a first-party shipped role binds a genuinely distinct model-backed Reviewer to independent original code evidence; review does not merely repeat implementer self-report. The binding phase sequence is first-party skill instruction rather than hard backend enforcement. - Claim being audited: implementer has delivered requirements-compliant work with passing tests/ready for approval. - Ordinary reporting path: Programmer/Tester results and step completion messages to Director. - Complementary access path: fresh Reviewer reads actual full Git diff, design spec and runs targeted read-only tests, returning explicit source-anchored issues. - Independence boundary: fresh model role has no code authorship/commit permission in the skill and sees diff not just implementer summary; no guarantee of independence across model weights/providers or perfect compliance. - Who acts on findings: model Director sends fixes to Programmer/Tester, repeats separate Reviewer gate and only then asks operator. - Evidence: cafleet/src/assets.rs; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/roles/reviewer.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/reference/coordination.md. - Basis: mode-bounded first-party installed skill autonomous audit return. - Confidence: medium; unobserved reviewer invocation and prompt compliance. - Caveats: not a Rust-enforced hard gate, no global automated quality guarantee, operator may use ordinary CAFleet as generic broker with no execute role.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped fleet-level externally informed prospective organizational capacity adaptation path established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: longer-term changes in coding provider capabilities, tool landscape and work requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide and implement future system adaptations based on external sensing, not evidenced in installed paths. - Decision owner: the human or model can pick member model/roles for current task, but this is deployment/current S3 rather than a separate prospective organ. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model catalog guidance, role presets, cost-mode choice, historical messages/design docs, per-fleet config. - Closure path: current task spawns may select models, but no proven external prospecting → changed future fleet capability → applied new organizational capacity feedback. - Boundary reachability: inspected bundled install, model selection, docs planning and monitor signals; these concern present execution or maintenance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model choosing a cheaper reviewer for this session is not long-horizon S4 future adaptation. - Evidence: skills/cafleet/roles/director.md; skills/cafleet/reference/coding-agents.md; cafleet/src/assets.rs; cafleet/src/monitor/mod.rs. - Basis: bounded negative after checking likely shipped paths. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: humans may create new skills/models outside assessed package.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: distinct legitimate fleet identity/ethos/ultimate policy choice and governing return not demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unresolved organizational purpose, ethical mandate and conflicting long-term versus current demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: settle ultimate organizational identity commitments and return new policy into the fleet; not evidenced. - Decision owner: operator/user owns publication and higher-authority decisions outside the broker; that is not automatically S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fleet registry/slug and Director identity, model backend choice, role instructions, human approval, command allow/deny/escalation. - Closure path: user approves changes or commands; no distinct ultimate policy issue and authority settlement governing both present and prospective functions. - Boundary reachability: shipped fleet create, identity, deny-command decision surface and Step 6 user approval inspected. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary code approval, permissions and naming do not establish model or parent-owned organizational ultimate identity regulation. - Evidence: skills/cafleet/roles/director.md; skills/cafleet-design-doc/execute/execute.md; cafleet/src/broker/fleets.rs; cafleet/src/cli/fleet.rs. - Basis: bounded negative for assessed recursion. - Confidence: medium-high within assessed mode. - Caveats: real human organizations necessarily have governance outside CAFleet, not credited as first-party S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/fc23ecd690f2c794a50166f730173dff5c54c1fc/assessments/cafleet.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit fc23ecd690f2c794a50166f730173dff5c54c1fc\">fc23ecd &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T19:16:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/anYuJia/yode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/anYuJia/yode/tree/964d6230afb5e779e3157a452dac1c0ac0d24f16\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 964d6230afb5e779e3157a452dac1c0ac0d24f16\">964d623 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing software-engineering work by inspecting code/state, choosing tools, editing files, running commands/tests, and revising actions from returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repository structure, incomplete task requirements, command/test failures, code and git state, provider responses, permissions, context pressure, and changing workspace evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding action, tool use, direct edit/command, delegation step, and completion response within the configured authority boundary. - Decision owner: the model-backed Yode AgentEngine actor; delegated sub-agents instantiate the same class of autonomous operational actor at lower recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry; context/session persistence; permissions; cancellation; retry/recovery hints; LSP/search/git/browser/MCP tools; sub-agent runner; desktop event/runtime state. - Closure path: user goal + workspace/context → model decision → first-party tool/sub-agent action → environment result/event → result returns into the model context → next coding decision or completion. - Boundary reachability: the supported desktop runtime directly instantiates this loop; downstream application code is not required to supply the core coding actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed engine leaves execution, persistence and safety machinery but removes the open-ended task-specific coding choices. - Evidence: README.md; crates/yode-core/src/engine.rs; crates/yode-core/src/engine/subagentrunner.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is external; the assessment credits Yode&#39;s first-party role/tool/feedback composition rather than provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 loop was established at the selected desktop/team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent model-backed team members can in principle contend over shared repository state, but the reviewed standard team/coordinator path does not itself connect that disturbance to an operational attenuation loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. The main agent may choose dependencies, maxparallel, or explicit worktree isolation, but those are delegation/scheduling/capability choices without a demonstrated shipped relation that detects or structurally maps an actual inter-S1 interference and feeds attenuation back into the affected S1 units. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency DAGs, bounded parallel batches, team messaging/state, optional explicit sub-agent isolation: &quot;worktree&quot;, standalone worktree tools, and a WorktreeCoordinator used by the separate multi-repository executor. - Closure path: not applicable at this recursion; no standard desktop/team path was found that takes a concrete inter-S1 conflict/oscillation, applies a coordination relation to it, and returns the result into subsequent behavior of the affected team S1s. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can decide how to delegate and can explicitly request isolation, but generic sequencing, concurrency limits, messaging and optional isolation capability do not by themselves establish the S2 function. - Evidence: crates/yode-agent/src/planning.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/coordinator/mod.rs; crates/yode-core/src/engine/subagentrunner.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/worktreecoordinator.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: yode-runtime::multirepo does use WorktreeCoordinator for mutating multi-repository steps, but PRODUCT.md places Multi-repo Agent in P2 and the reviewed supported desktop/team organization does not establish that as its normal inter-S1 coordination path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain a current view of the multi-agent coding organization and make task/worker execution decisions over active, ready, blocked, completed and failed work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing worker/task status, dependency readiness, background execution, failures, pending messages, returned workstream results and current orchestration progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the main model-backed agent can choose team/workstream decomposition, dependencies and concurrency, inspect current team state, send new guidance/handoffs, invoke ready-step execution, request/inspect background task output, and choose subsequent current-work interventions. - Decision owner: the main model-backed Yode agent using first-party team/coordinator/task tools. - Whole-system current view: teammonitor and persisted AgentTeamState expose the current team goal/plan plus member counts and member-level planned/running/completed/failed status, ready/blocked/running/completed/failed plan progress, runtime task IDs, result previews and pending team messages. - Current-control decision scope: the main agent can choose/decompose workstreams, dependencies and concurrency, launch or continue current team work through teamrunready / sub-agent tools, send mid-course guidance through sendmessage, inspect current/background results, and create follow-up work in response to failures or completion evidence. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTeamManager; persisted team state/monitor/bundle artifacts; teammonitor; teamrunready; sendmessage; runtime task store; dependency planner; deterministic status reconciliation. - Closure path: current team/task state is exposed to the main agent → the agent interprets current progress/failure/blocked state → selects a current-control action such as dispatching ready work, changing decomposition/concurrency, sending guidance or launching follow-up work → first-party runtime updates worker/team state → the resulting state/output returns for subsequent decisions. - Boundary reachability: team/coordinator tools are part of the shipped desktop agent tool surface and sub-agent runtime; they are not merely an external SDK substrate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic managers expose/enforce state, but removing the main model-backed agent removes the discretionary current-work decisions over what to delegate, how to sequence/parallelize, and how to respond to live results. - Evidence: README.md; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/coordinator/mod.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/teamruntime/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: the deterministic DAG executor alone is not credited as S3 ownership. The positive classification depends on the main model actor&#39;s access to current team state plus its first-party intervention tools.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation claims and return findings into a path that can block delivery/commit or trigger corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: false completion, regressions, missing test coverage, risky assumptions, build/test failures and implementation errors not caught by the implementing S1. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separate model-backed review/verification agent inspects current workspace evidence and produces findings/judgment; that judgment determines whether the review pipeline may proceed to commit absent explicit override. - Decision owner: the dedicated model-backed verification/review sub-agent. - Claim being audited: that the implementation/current workspace changes are correct and safe enough to proceed toward delivery or commit. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementing main/worker S1 returns its own completion text, tool results and team/task result state through the normal coding loop. - Complementary access path: verificationagent, reviewchanges, reviewpipeline, and reviewthencommit launch a separate sub-agent that directly inspects git/workspace state and may run targeted tests/commands with a review-specific tool allowlist. - Independence boundary: the audit actor is a fresh model-backed AgentEngine/sub-agent with a distinct review or verification prompt, conversation context and restricted evidence-gathering tool surface; it is not the implementing S1 merely rereading its own conclusion. - Who acts on findings: the first-party review pipeline deterministically returns a recoverable validation failure and skips commit by default; the parent/main agent receives the findings/error and can correct the implementation and rerun the audit. - Boundary reachability: the dedicated verification/review tools and sub-agent runner are shipped in the first-party yode-tools / yode-core runtime used by the supported desktop composition; no downstream application-authored auditor is required. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated sub-agent runner; restricted inspection/test tool set; persisted review artifacts; findings parsing/counting; deterministic review-pipeline gate; reviewthencommit commit suppression. - Closure path: implementation exists → fresh verification/review sub-agent inspects repository/git/test evidence → findings are returned to the parent/tool pipeline → findings cause a recoverable validation failure and commit is skipped by default → subsequent agent work can address findings before retrying verification/commit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runtime gate is deterministic support, but the complementary audit judgment is produced by a separate model-backed actor with direct workspace/test access rather than by the implementing S1. - Evidence: crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/verificationagent/mod.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/reviewpipeline/execution.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/reviewthencommit/execution.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: confirmation requirements constrain execution authority but do not move the audit judgment away from the independent verification/review actor.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Yode records postmortems, failures, verification evidence and lessons, performs current-run recovery hints, supports repository/web intelligence and can persist memory; these mechanisms do not close a future-oriented adaptation loop that changes organizational capability/strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LearningStore; postmortem/lesson persistence; current-run failure/retry hints; repository intelligence/indexes; memory/instruction loading; evaluation artifacts. - Closure path: no standard path was found from external/future distinctions through adaptation-option selection into a persistent change of Yode&#39;s current organizational capability. At the frozen ref, relevantlessons() is defined in the learning store but is not consumed by the operational agent runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: retrospective lesson derivation and present-run recovery can improve continuity/behavior without constituting the required outside-and-future intelligence function. - Evidence: crates/yode-core/src/learning.rs; crates/yode-core/src/engine/intelligenceruntime.rs; crates/yode-core/src/instructions.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: README language such as “Evaluate / Postmortem / Learn” is not promoted to S4 without operational prospective-adaptation closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity or ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user, managed, project and local instruction/permission layers constrain tool authority; plan-mode approval and per-tool confirmations constrain execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an identity/ultimate-policy issue. Managed/user configuration can set stronger operating constraints, but no runtime S5 actor receives an identity-level issue, decides the organization&#39;s governing purpose/policy, and returns that decision into later operation. - Decision owner: none established inside the assessed Yode organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: managed/user/project/local permission layers; project instructions; plan-mode approval; tool confirmation requirements; workspace trust and deny rules. - Closure path: no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate S5 authority → authoritative policy decision → returned governance path was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: static or externally edited configuration and permission enforcement define the operating envelope but do not themselves constitute runtime S5 ownership. - Evidence: apps/yode-desktop/src-tauri/src/runtime/turnpermissions.rs; crates/yode-core/src/instructions.rs; crates/yode-tools/src/builtin/planmode/mod.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic approvals and managed policy are important governance/safety mechanisms, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not promote them to S5 without function-specific identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0039698c28470f5bd69869a7a46a2582ff638021/assessments/yode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0039698c28470f5bd69869a7a46a2582ff638021\">0039698 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-29T00:34:02+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sipyourdrink-ltd/bernstein\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bernstein</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-29\">2026-09-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/sipyourdrink-ltd/bernstein/tree/c3d317385a058ae2b5f478399373d09d84d80962\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c3d317385a058ae2b5f478399373d09d84d80962\">c3d3173 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive autonomous task work inside a governed Bernstein run through a supported first-party agent execution mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended coding/research tasks, target-repository state, tool/MCP observations, model outputs, execution failures and returned verification feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: interpret the assigned task and current observations, choose model/tool actions, evaluate returned tool results and continue/revise until an operational result is produced. - Decision owner: the autonomous agent instantiated through Bernstein&#39;s supported openaiagents runner mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bernstein spawner, runner manifest, role/model policy, sandbox provider, MCP bridging, session/lifecycle handling, timeouts, cost tracking, task server and lineage/audit instrumentation. - Closure path: scheduler admits task → Bernstein builds runner manifest and launches first-party openaiagentsrunner → runner constructs SDK Agent with model/tools/sandbox/MCP context → Runner.runsync performs model/tool turns → completion/tool/session events return through Bernstein → task result enters verification and subsequent task operation. - Boundary reachability: openaiagents is a documented built-in Bernstein adapter selectable from normal plan.yaml; the optional SDK extra is an explicitly supported dependency, and the runner/manifest/lifecycle integration is first-party at the frozen revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous Agent/Runner actor while leaving scheduler, worktrees, claims and gates intact leaves task transport/enforcement but removes the substantive open-ended operational decision loop. External CLI wrappers are not needed for this witness. - Evidence: docs/adapters/openai-agents.md; src/bernstein/adapters/openaiagents.py; src/bernstein/adapters/openaiagentsrunner.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Claude/Codex and similar terminal-agent integrations may place more of the autonomous loop in adjacent CLI products. The A finding relies on the separately documented SDK-backed first-party execution mode, not on inheriting autonomy from every adapter Bernstein can spawn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent worker interference by preventing overlapping file/task work from being admitted simultaneously and by isolating admitted work in per-agent worktrees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more operational workers may otherwise modify the same or inferred-overlapping repository paths concurrently, producing collision, stale assumptions, conflicting changes or unsafe landing order. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a candidate task batch is safe to spawn given current file ownership and active worktree modifications, and withhold conflicting work until the disturbance clears. - Decision owner: deterministic Bernstein task-lifecycle/scheduler machinery; no autonomous coordination agent owns the overlap judgment in the standard mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: explicit ownedfiles, inferred affected paths, fileownership, active worktree diff inspection, per-agent worktrees, file locks, merge serialization, dirty-target/read-set/conflict refusal. - Closure path: scheduler considers ready task/batch → first-party lifecycle compares its paths with live-agent ownership and active worktree changes → overlap causes spawn suppression → non-overlapping workers proceed in isolated worktrees → changed ownership/worktree state is reconsidered on later ticks → later S1 admission therefore changes from the coordination result. - Boundary reachability: overlap checks execute in ordinary task claim/spawn lifecycle and worktree isolation is a standard Bernstein execution mechanism, not an example-only constructor. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently runnable autonomous worker tasks instantiated through supported agent execution modes, each with its own task identity/worktree and operational objective. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous edits to the same or inferred-overlapping repository paths can create collision/conflict and invalidate assumptions between independently acting workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: Bernstein withholds a candidate batch when ownedfiles, inferred paths, lock-manager ownership or active worktree diffs overlap, while isolated worktrees and serialized merge paths keep admitted work separated until landing. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a suppressed task is not spawned in that tick; as active ownership/worktree state changes, later scheduling can admit it, changing which autonomous worker acts next. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the witness is a concrete anti-collision relation over plural operational units and shared repository paths, not merely task queues, dependencies or parallelism. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the first-party mechanism establishes the S2 function and closes the attenuation feedback, but the overlap decision is a fixed deterministic policy. No autonomous actor is shown deciding or adapting the coordination relation, so constructor C is published. - Evidence: src/bernstein/core/tasks/tasklifecycle.py; src/bernstein/core/git/incrementalmerge.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: dependency ordering, task claiming, shared whiteboard and ordinary message transport are not independently credited as S2. The positive witness is specifically the live overlap/isolation feedback path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: review the live whole-run task organization and autonomously revise current commitments, roles and priorities when completions, failures or stalls indicate the current operating plan needs correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task completion/failure state, stalled work, unsuitable role assignment, wrong priority, obsolete commitments, newly necessary tasks and remaining budget pressure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose queue corrections among reassigning role, cancelling work, changing priority and adding a task, based on the current queue and budget/completion/failure context. - Decision owner: first-party LLM-powered ManagerAgent during periodic queue review. - Whole-system current view: the manager review receives the current open, claimed and failed task queue together with completion/failure counters and remaining budget context for the governed run. - Current-control decision scope: role reassignment, task cancellation, priority change and creation of newly necessary work across the live run, with deterministic validation/application by the orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task-server snapshot, manager prompt/typed QueueReviewResult, completion/failure counters, stall timer, budget tracker, correction validation and deterministic task-server PATCH/POST application. - Closure path: run accumulates three completions, any failure, explicit review request or stall interval → orchestrator instantiates ManagerAgent with current run context → manager reviews open/claimed/failed queue and returns typed corrections → orchestrator validates/applies them to task-server state → deterministic scheduler sees changed roles/priorities/cancellations/new tasks → subsequent worker commitments change. - Boundary reachability: manager queue review is wired into the ordinary orchestrator slow tick and uses shipped thresholds; it is not a documentation-only manager pattern. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the deterministic orchestrator enforces and validates corrections, but the discretionary judgment over which current correction to make is produced by the ManagerAgent. Removing that model judgment leaves counters and enforcement but not the same live organizational correction choice. - Evidence: src/bernstein/core/orchestration/manager.py; src/bernstein/core/orchestration/managermodels.py; src/bernstein/core/orchestration/orchestrator.py. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect completed operational work through a reviewer path that is distinct from the worker&#39;s ordinary production/reporting path and return a corrective judgment before merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a worker can report completion while its actual diff contains correctness, security, bug, style or scope defects that ordinary completion claims/signals may miss. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the concrete git diff and task intent using a reviewer model different from the writer, then decide approve versus requestchanges; in blocking mode the latter prevents merge and creates/feeds corrective work. - Decision owner: the autonomous cross-model reviewer (or configured reviewer quorum) invoked by Bernstein&#39;s first-party verifier. - Claim being audited: the worker&#39;s substantive completion claim that its produced repository change correctly satisfies the assigned task and is suitable to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: the worker exits/completes and reports its task result through Bernstein&#39;s normal agent/session completion path, including its own output and completion signals. - Complementary access path: Bernstein independently extracts the concrete git diff from the worker worktree, scoped to task-owned files where declared, and sends that artifact plus task intent to a distinct reviewer model. - Independence boundary: first-party reviewer selection maps writer families to a different reviewer model/provider axis and rejects an explicit reviewer override that collides with the writer model; optional quorum can widen that separation further. - Who acts on findings: Bernstein&#39;s completion/merge-control path acts on requestchanges; with blocking enabled it prevents ordinary merge and routes the finding into corrective/fix work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff extraction, explicit writer→different-reviewer mapping, same-model override rejection, optional multi-model voting, conventions injection, blocking configuration, janitor/gate pipeline and corrective task/escalation machinery. - Closure path: worker finishes → Bernstein captures actual worktree diff/task scope → cross-model verifier selects a different reviewer model/provider axis and obtains an independent judgment → requestchanges with blocking enabled prevents normal merge and enters correction/fix handling → later S1 work therefore changes from the audit finding. - Boundary reachability: qualitygates.crossmodel.enabled: true is a documented first-party runtime configuration; the verifier is shipped and integrated into completion processing even though the orchestrator defaults it off. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic tests and janitor checks remain supporting evidence, but the credited audit judgment is made by a separate autonomous reviewer over complementary actual-diff evidence. The writer cannot satisfy this path merely by asserting success. - Evidence: docs/architecture/quality-pipeline.md; src/bernstein/core/quality/crossmodelverifier.py; src/bernstein/core/quality/janitor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the cross-model verifier is fail-open on some reviewer-call/parse failures and is disabled by default. Those are robustness/default-policy limitations, not absence of the supported autonomous audit mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: turn future/external or trend-relevant evidence about the operating environment into concrete proposals for changing Bernstein&#39;s present routing, model, policy, role-template or provider capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: cost/quality/failure trends and external repository-health regressions can make the current model routing, policy, role templates or provider setup unsuitable for later runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select an adaptation proposal and admit it into an approval/execution path that can change current capability. - Decision owner: not closed by a first-party autonomous S4 actor in the standard mode; detector/proposal/executor logic is primarily deterministic and configuration/risk driven. - External distinction: when composed with observabilitysnapshotsdir, the detector compares successive external repository-health snapshots and distinguishes new security/coverage regressions from the prior observed environment. - Future / prospective distinction: the detector also treats persistent cost, success-rate and failure-pattern trends as evidence that the current routing/model/template/provider arrangement may be unsuitable for subsequent runs, not merely as a report about the completed run. - Adaptation option generated: typed proposals can change policy, routing rules, model routing, role templates or provider configuration, with explicit risk, expected-improvement and rollback metadata. - Path back into current capability / S3: approved proposals enter the evolution executor, which can apply the configured change (or roll it back on failure), thereby altering the capability/configuration available to later current-operation scheduling and execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MetricsAggregator, OpportunityDetector, failure-pattern analysis, observability-snapshot regression detection, ProposalGenerator, risk/approval modes, EvolutionCoordinator, upgrade executor, rollback/circuit/invariant machinery. - Closure path: first-party constructor can read task/cost/failure trends and, when explicitly composed with observabilitysnapshotsdir, external security/coverage regressions → detector emits typed ImprovementOpportunity → proposal generator maps it to a concrete policy/routing/model/template/provider change → evolution approval/executor can apply/rollback the change → later operating capability can differ. - Boundary reachability: the evolution package is shipped/exported and its detector explicitly accepts the observability snapshot corpus, but the default AnalysisEngine constructs OpportunityDetector without that external snapshot input; a developer/operator must therefore compose the outside-sensing path and autonomous adaptation authority to close S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the repository supplies a function-specific outside/trend-to-adaptation constructor and execution return path, but its decisive adaptation selection/approval is heuristic/configured rather than autonomously owned. This is narrower than awarding S4 for generic learning, metrics or a roadmap. - Evidence: src/bernstein/evolution/detector.py; src/bernstein/evolution/proposals.py; src/bernstein/evolution/init.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: internally observed success/cost metrics alone are not treated as sufficient outside-and-then intelligence. The positive constructor relies on the explicit external observability-regression input plus the shipped adaptation proposal/execution machinery; default wiring remains incomplete.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish the authoritative governance playbook that defines forbidden, required and permitted posture for later Bernstein runs, while keeping the ultimate enactment right at the legitimate parent human/operator. - Disturbance / variety regulated: observed governance findings may imply a new desired policy/posture, but a model-generated suggestion must not silently redefine what the governed organization permits or requires. - Decisive decision or feedback right: sign or refuse the exact governance playbook artifact that will govern subsequent runs. - Decision owner: legitimate parent human/operator signing a content-specific governance proposal. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which governance posture the Bernstein organization will treat as forbidden, required and permitted for future runs, rather than whether one current task should pass a local approval. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: in the claimed parent-governed mode, the human/operator who signs the exact content-addressed governance playbook is the ultimate authority; the drafting model has proposal authority only and cannot enact the policy. - Return-to-operation path: govern apply refuses unsigned drafts, while a human-signed playbook is admitted by hash and is then consumed by later Bernstein runs, so subsequent governed operation executes under the returned parent policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: chain-grounded findings document, model-assisted DraftProposal, canonical content hash, recorded prompt/model/findings digests, proposal status/signature, govern apply refusal for unsigned drafts and run consumption of the signed playbook by hash. - Closure path: first-party governance discovery observes chain-recorded findings → optional model drafts a forbidden/required/permitted playbook proposal → human reviews and signs the exact content hash (or refuses) → unsigned apply is rejected; signed playbook is accepted → later Bernstein runs consume that signed playbook → subsequent operation is governed by the parent decision. - Boundary reachability: ADR-011 is an accepted first-party architectural decision describing the shipped govern discover --assist / DraftProposal / govern apply contract and explicitly states that the resulting signed playbook governs future runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may draft policy text but cannot enact it; the repository explicitly makes the human signature the authoritative act and rejects auto-acceptance as a signature. Thus the function is positively closed but parent-owned, yielding P, not A/C. - Evidence: src/bernstein/core/govern/proposal.py; docs/decisions/011-model-drafts-human-signs.md; src/bernstein/cli/commands/governancecmd.py. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: ordinary RBAC checks, budgets, quality gates, task approvals and static bernstein.yaml configuration are not independently credited as S5. The positive witness is specifically the signed future-run governance-playbook authority path.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bbcea78e47704d914f485ad3cee1d1c080807493/assessments/bernstein.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bbcea78e47704d914f485ad3cee1d1c080807493\">bbcea78 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T20:55:47Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pydantic AI Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic-ai-harness/tree/434649f87d34aa7e5d48c7bd26ad33c50d57cf10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 434649f87d34aa7e5d48c7bd26ad33c50d57cf10\">434649f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously execute a bounded coding/research task using first-party Harness tools, workspace/context capabilities and model-driven action selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/web/task ambiguity, file and shell state, tool results, test failures, retrieved evidence, long-horizon context pressure and intermediate errors encountered while completing the task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive investigation/tool/edit/research action and iterate from resulting observations until the assigned task is completed or otherwise concluded. - Decision owner: the model-driven Pydantic AI Agent configured and exported through the Harness&#39;s first-party Coder/Researcher path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Coder/Researcher combined capabilities, filesystem/shell/web tools, repository context, context compaction/output limits, memory, guardrails/spend controls and the Pydantic AI Agent execution substrate. - Closure path: user task → packaged Harness-enabled Agent → model-selected tool/actions → file/shell/web/tool observations → further model decisions → result/changes/evidence. - Boundary reachability: the repository ships a runnable pydanticaiharness.coder:coderagent and documents zero-setup CLI execution; users may also instantiate Agent(..., capabilities=[Coder()]) directly. The assessed actor is therefore wired into a supported first-party Harness mode rather than existing only in examples/dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: first-party capabilities constrain and expose actions, but the autonomous model decides which actions to take from current operational evidence; deterministic tool/runtime machinery does not preselect the task solution. - Evidence: README.md, pydanticaiharness/coder/agent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Pydantic AI core provides the generic Agent loop as a dependency; this assessment does not import unrelated core metasystem capabilities. S1 credit rests on this repository&#39;s shipped concrete Harness agent/capability composition and documented autonomous operating path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently evidenced S2-specific inter-S1 conflict/oscillation attenuation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no concrete cross-S1 interference witness meeting the Profile threshold is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for an S2-specific attenuation relation. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SubAgents and DynamicWorkflow provide named delegation, isolation, fan-out, chaining, voting, retry control and result transport; budgets bound child execution. - Closure path: no qualifying inter-S1 conflict/oscillation → S2-specific attenuation → returned change in later S1 behaviour loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model can coordinate in the ordinary software sense, but the Methodology requires more than routing/delegation/sequencing/shared results. Parallel alternatives, critic scoring and workflow dependencies do not by themselves prove that distinct S1 units are interfering and that a separate coordination relation attenuates that interference. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/dynamicworkflow/README.md, README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after function-first review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a downstream workflow can define a real S2 disturbance and relation, but general DynamicWorkflow expressiveness is not enough for a repository-level positive S2 claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: in DynamicWorkflow mode, regulate the current whole-team portfolio of subordinate agent calls, dependencies, retries and result-conditioned follow-up commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing intermediate subagent results, failed review outcomes, alternative candidate quality, fan-out resource needs and dependencies that require deciding which child work to launch, retry, chain, select or stop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the workflow program that allocates child-agent calls and conditionally changes subsequent team commitments based on current returned results. - Decision owner: the parent orchestration agent model, which writes the runworkflow Python program from the current task and available subagent catalog. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DynamicWorkflow capability, Monty sandbox, named-agent catalog, isolated child Agent.run calls, asyncio fan-out, result values, hard maxagentcalls and usage/resource limits. - Closure path: current task + available team catalog → parent model writes workflow → child S1 runs return live results → model-authored program branches/selects/retries/dispatches later children → final team output returns to parent operation. - Boundary reachability: DynamicWorkflow is a shipped documented Harness capability. Supplying a catalog of named Pydantic AI agents gives the parent model the standard runworkflow tool; no downstream scheduler/control-plane implementation is required for the autonomous parent to write and execute current-control logic. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the sandbox and budgets deterministically execute/enforce the generated program, but the discretionary whole-team choreography and conditional intervention logic is authored by the parent model for the current task. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/dynamicworkflow/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this positive S3 applies to the supported DynamicWorkflow team mode, not every single-agent Coder run. Preconfigured hard budgets and sandbox resource limits are supporting enforcement, not the ownership basis. - Whole-system current view: the generated workflow program holds the set of team members it can call and receives intermediate results from all child runs used in that workflow, allowing later calls/selection/retry to depend on current team outcomes. - Current-control decision scope: allocate/fan out child calls, sequence dependencies, compare/select candidate outputs, retry failed/reviewed work and launch follow-up agents from current intermediate evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review the live operational trajectory with a second model and return corrective steering into the running production agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: instruction drift, unsupported claims, scope creep, hallucination/security concerns and other run-level deviations that may not be detected by the production agent&#39;s ordinary self-report. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge the bounded recent trajectory as AllGood or produce a specific Steer(message) correction. - Decision owner: the separate model-driven TrajectoryJudge agent/model instance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: cadence/window hooks, bounded transcript rendering, concurrent judge evaluation, enforced verdict output type, RunContext.enqueue(..., &#39;asap&#39;), attribution and run usage/failure accounting. - Closure path: live production trajectory → separate judge receives raw recent transcript/tool evidence → independent AllGood/Steer verdict → Steer enqueued to production conversation → next model request receives correction → subsequent production behaviour changes. - Boundary reachability: TrajectoryJudge is a shipped documented capability that can be added directly to an Agent with its own model/instructions or a full distinct judge Agent; the steering enqueue path is supplied by the capability itself. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment is made by a second autonomous model rather than by deterministic validation or the same production actor; the runtime only schedules the review and transports the verdict. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/trajectoryjudge/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the judge sees a rendered recent trajectory rather than independently executing the environment itself; the complementary path is direct access to user/assistant/tool-call/tool-result evidence rather than relying on the production agent&#39;s final summary. - Claim being audited: the live agent run remains on-task, evidence-grounded and otherwise compliant with the judge&#39;s specified concern. - Ordinary reporting path: the production agent&#39;s own messages, final output and normal task/tool loop. - Complementary access path: the judge receives a bounded raw transcript including user messages, assistant messages, tool calls and tool results on an independent cadence while the run is still active. - Independence boundary: a separate model or full Agent instance owns its own instructions/model configuration and cannot be replaced by the production agent&#39;s self-evaluation without removing the credited judge actor. - Who acts on findings: the Harness capability enqueues Steer into the running production conversation, and the production agent receives it on the next model request and changes subsequent action accordingly.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: expose a first-party path by which an agent can detect a missing host behavior during operational work, generate and validate a new reusable capability, and persist that adaptation for future activation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task/environment requirements reveal that the current host lacks a needed guardrail, instruction, tool or lifecycle hook that would improve future operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what new capability to author and generate its Python implementation from the observed need; activation of that authored capability in later operation still requires host/orchestrator composition. - Decision owner: the running agent owns adaptation-option generation; the complete future-capability closure is not autonomous because the surrounding orchestrator/application must load and pass active authored capabilities into later runs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: authorcapability, import/construction/static-getter validation, persistent source files + manifest.json, listing/disabling and store.loadactive(). - Closure path: current task exposes missing host behavior → agent authors/validates/persists capability → active capability store records option → documented host integration loads active capability into a subsequent agent.run → future capability changes. The final activation edge requires composition, which is why state is C rather than A. - Boundary reachability: CapabilityCreation is a shipped Harness capability with default agent-facing guidance and persistence/validation machinery. The S4-specific option-generation path is first-party; only the documented next-run activation loop remains for the developer/orchestrator to compose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: option discovery/authorship is agent-owned, but the library explicitly states that capabilities cannot enter the live run and that the orchestrator owns the integration contract for loading them on subsequent runs; therefore autonomous S4 closure is incomplete. - Evidence: pydanticaiharness/capabilitycreation/README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream application that implements the documented load-active loop may close autonomous S4 for that separate composed system-in-focus, but that composition is not credited back to this repository assessment. - External distinction: during a coding/operational task the agent discovers that the host lacks a needed behavior such as a guardrail, tool, instruction or hook. - Future / prospective distinction: authored capabilities cannot affect the already-running execution; they are explicitly intended for activation on the next run or a later loop iteration. - Adaptation option generated: a validated persistent AbstractCapability implementation authored by the agent and recorded in the capability store/manifest. - Path back into current capability / S3: store.loadactive() returns persisted active capabilities for the host to pass into a later agent.run; this is a function-specific return path but requires the host/orchestrator integration edge to close.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party identity / ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the reviewed agent-organization recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy disturbance established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system instructions, guardrails, spend/tool limits, tool approval, permission/configuration and capability composition constrain behaviour but do not constitute ultimate-policy ownership. - Closure path: no identity/policy issue → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned governance of subsequent organization loop established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-owned task planning/workflow control and self-extension remain operational/current-control/adaptation functions. They do not show the agent deciding the organization&#39;s ultimate identity/policy, and user-authored constraints do not become S5 merely because they are authoritative configuration. - Evidence: README.md, pydanticaiharness/capabilitycreation/README.md. - Basis: explicit absence after boundary review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: tool approval and operator configuration can represent legitimate human authority over particular actions without establishing a complete parent S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9828806eeec61c022133c2a70e42890196b90300/assessments/pydantic-ai-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9828806eeec61c022133c2a70e42890196b90300\">9828806 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T02:45:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SafeAI-Lab-X/ClawKeeper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ClawKeeper</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SafeAI-Lab-X/ClawKeeper/tree/69db077317bd8a89777c7670295539ed93145f46\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 69db077317bd8a89777c7670295539ed93145f46\">69db077 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: make a contextual safety decision for a proposed tool action and close that decision into the host execution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: benign versus malicious or drifted shell/filesystem/network/tool actions, prompt-injection consequences, credential access/exfiltration chains, contextual false positives and bypasses that require intent/history-sensitive judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose allow, ask, or deny for the proposed action after interpreting current intent, recent trajectory and deterministic evidence. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher role in the enabled Watcher mode; deterministic guards/post-filter constrain and can override that decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic guard chain; Watcher prompt; post-filter; session history; host adapter; pre-tool hook; HTTP Watcher client. - Closure path: host proposes tool call → deterministic findings + intent/history reach Watcher → model-backed safety proposal → deterministic post-filter → adapter returns block/allow result → host skips or proceeds with the tool operation. - Boundary reachability: the Watcher daemon/client and host adapter are shipped first-party integration surfaces; downstream users configure a model endpoint but do not need to invent the safety role or enforcement feedback path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the Watcher model, deterministic guards still enforce known patterns but cannot make the same contextual judgment over intent and recent trajectory. The model therefore owns the open-ended safety discretion in this first-party mode. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py; clawkeepercore/adapters/hermes.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: host-agent task reasoning is not credited. S1 is ClawKeeper&#39;s own safety operation, not the external coding operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material same-recursion inter-S1 coordination function was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple guard modules and external tool calls coexist, but they are stages/inputs in one safety path rather than distinct autonomous S1 units whose interaction produces an evidenced interference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S2. - Decision owner: not established as a first-party S2 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordered guard chains; deterministic strictest-wins post-filter; per-session history; adapter routing. - Closure path: guard findings are combined inside one safety decision path; no separate inter-S1 conflict→coordination→changed S1-behavior loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: aggregation, routing and arbitration among guard outputs do not by themselves establish S2. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: coordination among external agents protected by one deployment would belong to that host organization unless independently closed by ClawKeeper.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current protected agent trajectory as a whole and intervene in the next tool action when its relation to intent/history or current risk becomes unsafe. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multi-step drift, attack chains that become suspicious only across recent calls, mismatch between stated intent and proposed action, current deterministic findings and accumulated session context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: at each proposed tool call, use session-level intent and recent-call state to permit the current trajectory to continue, require escalation, or halt the action before execution. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher role, subject to stricter deterministic post-filter vetoes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded per-session history; remembered intent; deterministic findings; Watcher prompt; fail-safe; post-filter; blocking pre-tool hook. - Closure path: current session intent + recent tool history + proposed next action → Watcher whole-session safety judgment → deny/ask/allow → host pre-tool enforcement changes the next operation → the call is appended to session history for the following current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: Watcher history, evaluator, client and Hermes pretoolcall enforcement are shipped and wired as a supported optional ClawKeeper mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic rules can veto known hardline cases, but contextual current-trajectory intervention depends on model judgment over the whole recent session. Removing the model removes that discretionary supervisory choice. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/tools.py; clawkeepercore/adapters/hermes.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is safety current-control over the protected session, not task prioritization for the external host agent. - Whole-system current view: stated intent, bounded recent tool-call trajectory, proposed next call and current deterministic guard findings for the protected session. - Current-control decision scope: whether the session may execute the proposed next tool action, must ask/escalate, or must block it now.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: on-demand audit and scanners provide materially different evidence, but no standard first-party feedback path automatically or authoritatively closes those findings back into current control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: configuration weaknesses, dangerous skills, historical log patterns and control-catalogue findings can be detected outside the routine pre-tool path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established as a closed S3 audit judgment that necessarily returns into subsequent current regulation. - Decision owner: audit findings are deterministic; remediation requires a separate operator/client invocation of maintenance or manual action. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /v1/audit; log/skill scanners; finding scores; harden; rollback; next-step recommendations. - Closure path: audit/scanner reads independent state/history → returns findings/recommendations; any transition into harden or manual remediation is a separate external action, so the required findings→control closure is not first-party-complete. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the evidence path is complementary, but observability plus an optional separate remediation API is insufficient to credit a closed S3 function. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/audit.py; clawkeepercore/scanner.py; clawkeepercore/maintenance.py; clawkeepercore/server.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a host/operator composition can close this loop; that external composition is not credited to ClawKeeper alone.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: learn a new future-facing deterministic guard from an observed external attack/bypass pattern and return it into live safety capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: novel malicious commands or close variants that evade the existing deterministic guard repertoire but are recognized contextually by the Watcher. - Decisive decision or feedback right: generalize a confidently detected bypass into a candidate regex/guard target and decide whether it is specific enough to become a persistent future guard. - Decision owner: the model-backed Watcher synthesis role generates the adaptation; deterministic validation rejects invalid, duplicate or benign-overmatching candidates before activation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: high-confidence missed-guard trigger; synthesis prompt; benign corpus; regex compilation; LearnedPatternStore; guard-target classification; hot-reload bridge; optional upstream fingerprint sharing. - Closure path: external proposed call bypasses deterministic guards → Watcher denies/asks with high confidence → model synthesizes a generalized pattern → validation/persistence accepts it → applylearnedpatterns() mutates live guard rules → future calls are regulated by the new capability. - Boundary reachability: learning, persistence and hot reload are called directly by the shipped Watcher evaluator when the trigger condition holds; no downstream glue is required beyond enabling Watcher mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic memory is not credited; the positive witness is the model&#39;s prospective generalization of a newly observed threat into a reusable guard option that is automatically returned to present capability. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/watcher/agent.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/learner.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/reload.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: optional upstream sharing is not needed for the local S4 closure; the credited loop is local learning from externally presented attack variety. - External distinction: an action that passed all current deterministic guards but is judged dangerous by the contextual Watcher. - Future / prospective distinction: the synthesized regex is explicitly required to match the observed command and close variants, so it is a guard for future related actions rather than a record of the single episode. - Adaptation option generated: a model-generated candidate pattern plus target guard class and confidence, bounded by benign-corpus and regex validation. - Path back into current capability / S3: accepted candidates are persisted and immediately hot-reloaded into live deterministic guard modules used by subsequent pre-tool safety decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy closure was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: safety policy, confirmation thresholds, guard rules and fail-safe behavior constrain execution but remain ordinary operational safety policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue. - Decision owner: configuration/operator for policy values; Watcher and deterministic guards apply those values within current safety operation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: default policy; deterministic hardline vetoes; config; guard rules; operator confirmation path. - Closure path: not applicable at S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a safety filter or policy file does not become S5 merely because it has final veto power over ordinary tool calls. - Evidence: clawkeepercore/judge.py; clawkeepercore/watcher/policy.py. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a parent organization may treat some security policy as identity-level governance; that higher-recursion authority is not a first-party ClawKeeper function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a070822fa4ce9b9dc6fda1c62a7c3cb6a37697e2/assessments/clawkeeper.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a070822fa4ce9b9dc6fda1c62a7c3cb6a37697e2\">a070822 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-17T13:04:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SethGammon/Citadel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Citadel</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-17\">2026-10-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/SethGammon/Citadel/tree/38f3a4e7fa13cd48cfbedde872778b847af6c842\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 38f3a4e7fa13cd48cfbedde872778b847af6c842\">38f3a4e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding, research, review or test tasks in the target repository through first-party scoped agent roles and skills. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requirements, actual repository state, tool errors and test results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the configured coding model chooses and revises substantive code/research/tool steps to fulfill each bounded task. - Decision owner: provider-backed agent operating bundled Citadel skills/prompts; provider internal inference and generic vendor tools are external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: /do routing, installed skill definitions, delegate assignments, Git worktree/checkpoint state, evidence and callbacks. - Closure path: user outcome → route to model-backed specialist or delegate → tool/source/test work → returned result and observed evidence → correction and delivery. - Boundary reachability: the declared pinned /do and /archon instructions are shipped plugin skills; model executor is separately installed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model, scripts can route/validate but do not solve arbitrary engineering work. - Evidence: skills/do/SKILL.md; skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/fleet/SKILL.md; skills/review/SKILL.md - Basis: direct installed skill invocation and source code -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent inter-agent interference by detecting overlapping writable scopes and holding a competing work claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous operational agents editing the same module/worktree or conflicting merge ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: allow a first-party scope claim only when findOverlap returns no existing colliding writable scope, otherwise throw SCOPEOVERLAP. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic coordination constructor; assignment choices still belong to model orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: claim registry, prefix-overlap checks, read-only exemptions, task/worktree isolation and workflow claim/release instructions. - Closure path: agents choose bounded scopes → claimScope compares active write owners → overlap aborts competing claim/feedback → orchestrator must re-scope/serialize work; release restores opportunity. - Boundary reachability: core claims module is source-reachable via project-local coordination tooling and shipped /archon/fleet steps rather than merely in a document. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: conflict resolution is a deterministic constructor, not independently model-owned S2, and no global lock guarantee follows. - Evidence: core/coordination/claims.js; core/coordination/instances.js; skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/fleet/SKILL.md - Basis: source-structural explicit collision mechanism - Confidence: medium, with non-atomic cross-process race caveat - Caveats: the lookup/write sequence has a TOCTOU race; this is bounded mitigation not complete exclusion. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrent model-backed fleet/campaign delegates with declared writable project paths. - Inter-S1 disturbance: competing work on overlapping source paths can overwrite or invalidate sibling changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: scope-overlap comparison and refusal before writing the new ownership claim; optional isolated worktrees. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: on SCOPEOVERLAP, competing work is held/re-scoped instead of legitimately continuing under the claimed scope. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: checks a material competing-write relation between operational agents and returns a refusal rather than just recording messages or tasks.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current allocation, accountability, intervention and recovery across the scoped Citadel campaign&#39;s operational tasks and delegates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled phases, failed verifications, scope collisions, cost/resource constraints and changing evidence about current delivery. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model Archon decides campaign phase order, delegate assignments, direction corrections, retries and escalation based on whole-campaign ledger and evidence. - Decision owner: model-backed Archon current controller; deterministic scripts/checkpoints/evidence tests are enforcement or support. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign/feature/decision logs, context handoff, scope-claims, phase validator returns, command/checkpoint/human gates. - Closure path: campaign state and active phase results → Archon model chooses intervention/redelegate/hold/advance → updates campaign and delegate action → subsequent verifier/status feedback. - Boundary reachability: explicit supported /archon skills instruct complete campaign read, phase choice, delegation, corrective and completion decisions, not just scheduling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the Archon model is conceptually removed, the state/gates cannot select revised goals, allocate priorities or choose rework among alternatives. - Evidence: skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/do/SKILL.md; core/coordination/claims.js; agents/phase-validator.md - Basis: first-party model-led shipped campaign mode - Confidence: medium-high for source-described model authority - Caveats: no separate parent-governed S3 mode credited merely because user can approve a blocking step. - Whole-system current view: active campaign, phase end conditions, feature/decision ledger, delegation/claim context, blockers and verification evidence. - Current-control decision scope: choose work owners/phase resources, rework, interruption, corrections, failure escalation and delivery closure.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: complementary independently judged audit of operational producer claims using direct reality evidence rather than re-parsing ordinary handoffs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: producer HANDOFF claims may be inconsistent with changed code or actual behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct direct-artifact reviewer could return findings to compel new producer work, but entire function-specific independently owned path is not proven. - Decision owner: unresolved: phase-validator checks handoff claims, read-only arch-reviewer can examine files and /review independently reads diff; their binding integration into campaign rework is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dedicated reviewer persona, five-pass review skill, phase-end evidence runner and phase validator. - Closure path: plausible independent reviewer real file/diff inspection → finding → Archon action/rework; inspected default requirement instead validates HANDOFF plus ordinary test gates. - Boundary reachability: all reviewer artifacts and /archon are packaged at exact source, yet distinct reviewer invocation and audit-specific correction path are not proven in a supported completed mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: read-only role designation or test gate is insufficient to declare a separate audit authority. - Evidence: agents/arch-reviewer.md; skills/review/SKILL.md; skills/archon/SKILL.md; core/verification/profiles.js - Basis: plausible in-boundary pathway without decisive independent closure - Confidence: medium confidence in explicit uncertainty - Caveats: do not infer mandatory external reviewer, S3 autonomy, or independent quality from phase validator alone. - Claim being audited: a phase&#39;s implementation and evidence really meet architectural, code/test and requested constraints. - Ordinary reporting path: delegate HANDOFF/phase completion and verification status to Archon. - Complementary access path: separate read-only reviewer can inspect actual source and changes; not proven tied to every qualifying phase. - Independence boundary: dedicated read-only reviewer is distinct as a persona but first-party review-to-rework call path cannot yet be established. - Who acts on findings: Archon can re-delegate remediation in general, but whether findings from a distinct audit alter phase advancement is unproven.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: explicit prospective adaptation of skill capabilities to future task/environment requirements using measured feedback and model-led exploration. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new/failing performance criteria, changed repository constraints, skill drift, unfamiliar target scores and recurring usability shortcomings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: in opt-in /evolve mode, model director generates hypotheses/options, chooses improvement attack axes, applies tested nonregressing skill edits and cross-pollinates confirmed patterns. - Decision owner: configured model director running shipped /evolve skill; operator sets target and approved rubric, exceptional rubric &#39;level-up&#39; returns to parent but no distinct closed parent S4 mode claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scoring/rubrics, scouting agents, isolated worktree experiments, belief model, evidence logs, verification oracle, commit/reload-skills and /learn pattern compilation. - Closure path: observed external/task scores → prospective hypothesis → independent scout → model selects future skill edit → tested modification/commit → reload or future session uses changed skill, with repeated reassessment. - Boundary reachability: shipped /evolve is user-invocable and explicitly names Phase 5 modification + Phase 6 evidence compilation + Phase 7 cross-skill application; requires approved rubric and configured provider. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model autonomously selects and applies evidence-backed future skill changes within its declared constraints; a static wiki or generator alone would not do this. - Evidence: skills/evolve/SKILL.md; skills/learn/SKILL.md; skills/do/SKILL.md - Basis: explicit skill-defined source-reachable prospective action loop - Confidence: medium; no witnessed trials or measured improvement - Caveats: this is conditional opt-in skill adaptation rather than default project control or automatically evolving the external model; rubric choice remains human prior policy. - External distinction: skill/target quality feedback from tasks and evaluation inputs plus actual test/regression results, not simply next action on current task. - Future / prospective distinction: updates stored skill behavior for future independent tasks and sibling skills, with pattern library cross-pollination. - Adaptation option generated: hypothesis-driven skill patch, verified axis change or transferable pattern from observed scores. - Path back into current capability / S3: verified edits to SKILL.md are committed and reloaded where supported or applied in the next model session, changing future task routing/behavior.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate organizational ethos/identity settlement path proven for the local project Citadel campaign. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fundamental project/fleet identity and normative tensions between current operations and adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: legitimate ultimate policy right and normative return to mission operations would be required; no separate material path identified. - Decision owner: human user chooses installation, project policy and approves risk/level-up; the model is not ultimate identity authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project-local configuration, hook permissions, risk governance, rubrics and controlled user decisions. - Closure path: permissions, checkpoints and human approval govern task/skill changes but do not constitute independent first-party whole-system ultimate-policy closure. - Boundary reachability: inspected /do/archon/evolve policy, project configuration, risk/manual gates and role prompts at pinned ref; no external organization borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval of risky actions and metric rubric edits is not S5, and no first-party S5 autonomous loop found. - Evidence: skills/archon/SKILL.md; skills/evolve/SKILL.md; core/config/identity.js; skills/do/SKILL.md; docs/OPERATIONCONTROL.md - Basis: bounded negative at local project operation boundary -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/48c335b7bb4ff17d555163ebb83764b8d1a42a37/assessments/citadel.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 48c335b7bb4ff17d555163ebb83764b8d1a42a37\">48c335b &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
